1. The problem caching is trying to solve
Imagine this Java application:
public User getUser(long id) {
    return userRepository.findById(id);
}
The code looks trivial.
But internally:
Client
   ↓
Java Application
   ↓
Connection Pool
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Database
   ↓
Query execution
   ↓
Disk / memory
   ↓
Database
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Java Application
   ↓
Client
The important observation:
The CPU executing your Java code is extremely fast compared with many operations required to retrieve data.
Your CPU may execute billions of cycles per second.
But getting data from another machine requires:
network traversal
+
kernel processing
+
database processing
+
locking
+
buffer/cache lookup
+
possibly storage access
So the fundamental caching idea is:
If the same data is requested repeatedly, don't repeatedly perform the expensive operation. Keep a copy closer to the consumer.
________________________________________
2. A simple example
Suppose:
GET /users/100
is requested:
10,000 times/sec
Database query:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 100;
If every request reaches the database:
10,000 requests/sec
        ↓
10,000 DB queries/sec
But suppose the result rarely changes.
We could do:
First request
     ↓
Database
     ↓
Store result in cache

Next 9,999 requests
     ↓
Cache
Now:
Database
   ↑
1 request

Cache
   ↑
9,999 requests
This is the fundamental reason caching exists.
________________________________________
3. But caching isn't just about databases
This is important.
Caching can happen at many levels:
CPU
 ↓
CPU Cache
 ↓
RAM
 ↓
OS Page Cache
 ↓
Application Cache
 ↓
Distributed Cache
 ↓
Database Buffer Pool
 ↓
SSD
 ↓
HDD
 ↓
Remote Storage
Almost every modern computer system uses caching.
Caching is not a special technique added by Redis.
Caching is a fundamental computer architecture principle.
