4. The deepest reason: locality
Caching works because programs usually exhibit locality of reference.
There are two important forms.
Temporal locality
If something was used recently, it is likely to be used again soon.
Example:
User 100
User 100
User 100
User 100
The same data keeps being accessed.
________________________________________
Spatial locality
If something was accessed, nearby data is likely to be accessed.
Example:
Array:

[100, 101, 102, 103, 104, 105]
If your program accesses:
array[100]
it may soon access:
array[101]
array[102]
array[103]
This is why CPUs don't fetch just one tiny value from RAM.
They work with cache lines.
________________________________________
5. Before understanding application caching, understand the CPU
Consider:
int x = a[i];
You might think:
CPU → RAM → get x
But modern CPUs have a hierarchy.
Conceptually:
CPU Core
   ↓
Registers
   ↓
L1 Cache
   ↓
L2 Cache
   ↓
L3 Cache
   ↓
RAM
   ↓
Storage
The closer the storage is to the CPU:
smaller
faster
more expensive per byte
The farther away:
larger
slower
cheaper per byte
________________________________________
6. Memory hierarchy
A simplified picture:
                 FASTEST
                    ▲
                    │
              Registers
                    │
