What you should be able to explain now
If someone asks:
Why does caching make a system faster?
Don't answer:
"Because cache is faster."
Your deeper answer should be:
A cache exploits locality by keeping frequently reused information closer to the consumer. This avoids repeatedly paying the latency and resource cost of the authoritative or expensive source. The benefit comes at the cost of additional memory, maintaining another copy of data, and dealing with staleness and consistency.
That's the level we're targeting.

Lesson 32.2 — Cache Architecture & Access Patterns
Today we answer one question:
When an application wants data, who talks to the cache, who talks to the database, and when does data move between them?
The cache itself is not the difficult part.
The difficult part is deciding who owns the read/write flow.
We will cover:
1. Cache-Aside
2. Read-Through
3. Write-Through
4. Write-Back
5. Refresh-Ahead
Then we'll compare them internally.
________________________________________
1. First build the basic architecture
Without a cache:
Client
  ↓
Java Application
  ↓
Database
With a cache:
Client
  ↓
Java Application
  ↓
Cache
  ↓
Database
But this diagram hides the important question:
Who is responsible for deciding what happens when the cache misses?
That's what the access patterns answer.
________________________________________
2. Pattern #1 — Cache-Aside
This is the most important pattern to understand first.
The application itself controls both:
Cache
Database
Architecture:
             ┌─────────┐
             │  Cache  │
             └────┬────┘
                  │
Java Application ─┤
                  │
             ┌────▼────┐
             │Database │
             └─────────┘
The cache doesn't automatically know about the database.
The application coordinates everything.
________________________________________
3. Cache-Aside READ
Suppose:
GET user:100
Application executes:
cache.get("user:100")
Case 1 — HIT
Application
    │
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    │ FOUND
    ▼
 User
Database isn't touched.
________________________________________
Case 2 — MISS
Application
    │
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    │ NOT FOUND
    ▼
 Database
    │
    ▼
  User
    │
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    ▼
Application
The application typically does:
User user = cache.get(key);

if (user == null) {
    user = database.findById(id);
    cache.put(key, user);
}

return user;
This is the basic Cache-Aside algorithm.
________________________________________
4. Why is it called "aside"?
Because the cache sits beside the database.
The application decides:
Should I read cache?
Should I read DB?
Should I populate cache?
The database doesn't know the cache exists.
The cache doesn't necessarily know the database exists.
The application is the coordinator.
________________________________________
5. Cache-Aside WRITE
Now suppose:
UPDATE user:100
Database contains:
name = Gopal
We change it to:
name = Krishna
A common strategy is:
Application
    │
    ▼
Database
    │
    ▼
Invalidate cache
So:
database.update(user);

cache.delete("user:100");
Why delete instead of immediately putting the new value?
Because the database is the authoritative source.
The next read:
cache.get()
gets a miss.
Then:
DB
 ↓
new value
 ↓
cache
________________________________________
6. Why Cache-Aside is so popular
Because it is simple and flexible.
Application has complete control.
For example:
Read:
Cache → DB

Write:
DB → invalidate cache
It works very naturally with:
Spring Boot
+
Redis
+
relational database
A typical architecture:
                  ┌───────────┐
                  │   Redis   │
                  └─────▲─────┘
                        │
                        │
Client → Spring Boot ───┤
                        │
                        ▼
                  ┌───────────┐
                  │ PostgreSQL│
                  └───────────┘
________________________________________
7. But Cache-Aside has an important weakness
The application must correctly implement the cache logic.
Every developer needs to understand:
cache.get()
cache.put()
cache.delete()
If one code path forgets to invalidate:
Database = NEW
Cache = OLD
Now users receive stale data.
The cache pattern itself doesn't magically solve consistency.
________________________________________
8. Cache-Aside concurrent miss
This is where things get interesting.
Suppose cache doesn't contain:
user:100
At exactly the same time:
1,000 requests
arrive.
All do:
cache.get("user:100")
All get:
MISS
Then:
1,000 requests
       ↓
1,000 database queries
This is the cache stampede / thundering herd problem.
             Cache
          ┌────┴────┐
          │  MISS   │
          └────┬────┘
               │
      ┌────────┼────────┐
      ↓        ↓        ↓
     DB       DB       DB
      ↑        ↑        ↑
     1000 concurrent requests
We'll study solutions later.
________________________________________
9. Pattern #2 — Read-Through
Now we change responsibility.
With Cache-Aside:
Application → Cache
Application → Database
With Read-Through:
Application → Cache → Database
The application only talks to the cache.
             ┌───────────┐
Application →│   Cache   │
             └─────┬─────┘
                   │
                   ▼
              Database
The cache knows how to load missing data.
________________________________________
10. Read-Through cache miss
Application asks:
cache.get("user:100")
Cache doesn't have it.
Instead of returning:
MISS
the cache itself does:
Cache
  ↓
Loader
  ↓
Database
  ↓
Cache
  ↓
Application
Conceptually:
Application
    │
    │ get(user:100)
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    │ miss
    ▼
  Loader
    │
    ▼
 Database
    │
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    ▼
Application
________________________________________
11. Why would we want this?
Because application code becomes simpler.
Instead of:
User user = cache.get(key);

if (user == null) {
    user = database.findById(id);
    cache.put(key, user);
}
the application can conceptually do:
User user = cache.get(key);
The cache abstraction handles loading.
________________________________________
12. Important distinction
Cache-Aside:
Application owns loading
Read-Through:
Cache owns loading
This is the most important distinction.
________________________________________
13. Read-Through doesn't eliminate the database
It changes who coordinates access.
Still:
Application
     ↓
Cache
     ↓
Database
The database remains authoritative.
The cache becomes an intelligent intermediary.
________________________________________
14. Pattern #3 — Write-Through
Now we're dealing with writes.
Suppose application executes:
update(user:100)
With Write-Through:
Application
     ↓
Cache
     ↓
Database
The application writes to the cache.
The cache synchronously writes to the database.
________________________________________
15. Write-Through flow
Application
    │
    ▼
  Cache
    │
    │ write
    ▼
 Database
    │
    ▼
 success
Only after the database write succeeds should the operation generally be considered successful.
Conceptually:
cache.put(user);
and internally:
cache
 ↓
database.update()
________________________________________
16. Why use Write-Through?
The goal is to keep cache and database synchronized more naturally.
Suppose:
Cache = Gopal
DB    = Gopal
Write:
Krishna
Flow:
Application
    ↓
Cache
    ↓
Database
After successful write:
Cache = Krishna
DB    = Krishna
Both are updated as part of the write path.
________________________________________
17. But Write-Through has a cost
Every write now involves the database.
So:
Application
   ↓
Cache
   ↓
Database
doesn't make the database disappear.
The cache provides benefits primarily around subsequent reads.
Also, writes become dependent on database availability.
If DB is down:
Cache write
    ↓
Database
    X
What should happen?
The write may fail.
________________________________________
18. Write-Through vs Cache-Aside
Cache-Aside
WRITE

Application
    ↓
Database
    ↓
Invalidate cache
Write-Through
WRITE

Application
    ↓
Cache
    ↓
Database
The ownership differs.
________________________________________
19. Pattern #4 — Write-Back
Now we make a much bigger change.
With Write-Through:
Application
    ↓
Cache
    ↓
Database
and the database write happens immediately.
With Write-Back:
Application
    ↓
Cache
Database update happens later.
________________________________________
20. Write-Back flow
Suppose:
price = 100
Application changes:
price = 120
Write:
Application
    ↓
Cache
    ↓
SUCCESS
Database may still contain:
100
while cache contains:
120
Later:
Cache
   ↓
Database
Database becomes:
120
________________________________________
21. Why would anyone accept this?
Performance.
Imagine:
100,000 writes/sec
Writing synchronously to the database for every operation may be expensive.
Instead:
100,000 writes
      ↓
fast cache
      ↓
batch / async persistence
      ↓
database
The cache absorbs the write workload.
________________________________________
22. Write-Back is fundamentally asynchronous
Think:
Application
     │
     ▼
   Cache
     │
     │ immediately returns
     ▼
Application

       ...later...

   Cache
     │
     ▼
  DB writer
     │
     ▼
 Database
This is powerful.
But dangerous.
________________________________________
23. The biggest Write-Back risk
What happens if the cache dies before persistence?
Application
      ↓
Cache
      ↓
DATA NOT YET IN DB
      X
   CACHE CRASH
The latest data may be lost.
Therefore Write-Back requires serious durability mechanisms.
Examples include:
write queue
persistent log
replication
disk-backed cache
acknowledgement strategy
recovery process
This is why Write-Back isn't simply:
"store it in Redis and write later"
at serious scale.
________________________________________
24. Write-Back and ordering
Suppose:
Write A:
balance = 100

Write B:
balance = 200
The database must eventually receive:
A → B
not:
B → A
Otherwise:
Final DB = 100
even though the latest state should be:
200
So Write-Back systems must consider:
ordering
+
durability
+
retries
+
duplicate writes
These concepts will connect later to Kafka, idempotency, and exactly-once discussions.
________________________________________
25. Pattern #5 — Refresh-Ahead
Now another interesting strategy.
Normally:
Cache
  ↓
TTL expires
  ↓
MISS
  ↓
Database
This creates latency at expiration.
Refresh-Ahead tries to refresh the data before it expires.
________________________________________
26. Example
Suppose:
TTL = 60 seconds
Cache contains:
user:100
expires in 60 sec
At:
50 seconds
system notices:
10 seconds remaining
It proactively refreshes:
Cache
 ↓
Database
 ↓
new value
 ↓
Cache
So clients continue seeing a warm cache.
________________________________________
27. Why is Refresh-Ahead useful?
For extremely hot data.
Suppose:
product:iphone
receives:
100,000 requests/sec
If it expires:
100,000 requests
     ↓
MISS
     ↓
Database
That's dangerous.
Refresh-Ahead tries to ensure:
100,000 requests
     ↓
Cache HIT
while one background refresh updates the data.
________________________________________
28. But Refresh-Ahead has a hidden cost
You may refresh data nobody requests anymore.
Example:
10 million cached objects
If you proactively refresh all of them:
10 million DB reads
even though perhaps:
only 1% are actually hot
So Refresh-Ahead should generally be selective.
The system needs some indication of:
hotness
access frequency
expiration time
________________________________________
29. Let's compare all five
Pattern	Read miss	Write path	Main advantage	Main risk
Cache-Aside	App loads DB	DB + invalidate cache	Simple, flexible	App must manage consistency
Read-Through	Cache loads DB	Depends on implementation	Simpler application	Cache becomes more complex
Write-Through	Cache serves/coordinates	Cache → DB synchronously	Cache stays current	Write latency
Write-Back	Cache	Cache → DB asynchronously	Very fast writes	Data loss/ordering complexity
Refresh-Ahead	Cache refreshes before expiry	Usually independent	Avoids hot-key misses	Unnecessary refreshes
________________________________________
30. The patterns can be combined
This is important.
These aren't mutually exclusive.
For example:
Read-Through
+
Write-Through
+
Refresh-Ahead
could coexist.
Or:
Cache-Aside
+
Refresh-Ahead
could be implemented.
Real systems don't always fit into one textbook box.
________________________________________
31. Cache-Aside + Write-Through are often confused
Let's make the distinction crystal clear.
Cache-Aside
Application explicitly performs:
cache.get()
database.get()
cache.put()
The cache is mostly a passive store.
________________________________________
Read-Through
Application performs:
cache.get()
and cache knows:
"If missing, call loader."
________________________________________
Write-Through
Application writes:
Cache
and cache synchronously propagates:
Database
________________________________________
Write-Back
Application writes:
Cache
and persistence happens asynchronously.
________________________________________
32. The consistency spectrum
We can visualize the patterns like this:
                  More synchronous
                        │
                        ▼
Cache-Aside
     │
Write-Through
     │
Write-Back
     │
     ▼
More asynchronous
But this isn't a strict universal ordering in every implementation; the key distinction is when the database is updated relative to the application's successful write.
________________________________________
33. What happens during a failure?
This is where architecture becomes interesting.
Cache-Aside failure
Cache DOWN
   ↓
Application
   ↓
Database
Potentially the application can continue using the database.
Performance decreases.
________________________________________
Read-Through failure
Application
   ↓
Cache DOWN
   X
Whether the application can fall back depends on the cache abstraction.
________________________________________
Write-Through failure
Application
   ↓
Cache
   ↓
Database DOWN
Write generally fails because persistence hasn't succeeded.
________________________________________
Write-Back failure
Application
   ↓
Cache
   ↓
SUCCESS
but database hasn't received the update.
If cache then fails:
DATA LOSS
unless durability mechanisms exist.
________________________________________
34. A very important architectural principle
Ask:
Is the cache authoritative or merely an optimization?
Cache as optimization
Database = source of truth
Cache = disposable copy
If cache disappears:
rebuild it
This is the easiest architecture to reason about.
________________________________________
Cache as authoritative/write buffer
Cache = latest state
Database = eventually updated
Now cache failure becomes much more serious.
This is common in Write-Back designs.
________________________________________
35. Why Cache-Aside is often a safe starting point
Suppose Redis crashes.
With:
Database = source of truth
Redis = cache
you can potentially:
Redis unavailable
      ↓
cache miss
      ↓
Database
Performance suffers, but correctness can survive.
That's a very valuable property.
Compare that with:
Write-Back
where the cache may contain writes that haven't reached the database.
Redis failure could mean:
lost data
unless carefully designed for durability.
________________________________________
36. Concurrency problem — two cache misses
Let's go deeper.
Suppose:
Cache: MISS
Two threads:
Thread A → DB
Thread B → DB
Both receive:
User 100
Both try:
cache.put(user:100)
Usually this is harmless if the values are identical.
But imagine the underlying data changes between reads.
Timeline:
T1: A reads DB → version 1
T2: DB updated → version 2
T3: B reads DB → version 2
T4: A writes cache → version 1
T5: B writes cache → version 2
Fine.
But reverse ordering:
T1: A reads version 1
T2: B reads version 2
T3: B writes cache version 2
T4: A writes cache version 1
Now:
Cache = OLD VERSION
This is a race condition.
Caching isn't just:
get/put
Concurrency matters.
________________________________________
37. Another race: update vs cache population
Consider:
T1: Request A → cache MISS
T2: Request A → reads DB version 1
T3: Update request → DB version 2
T4: Request A → puts version 1 into cache
Now:
DB = version 2
Cache = version 1
This is one of the fundamental cache consistency races.
We'll study these systematically in the cache invalidation lesson.
________________________________________
38. A useful timeline model
When analyzing any caching design, draw:
time →
────────────────────────────────────────────>

Request A
Request B
Database Write
Cache Read
Cache Write
Cache Delete
Then ask:
What value can each request observe?
This is much more powerful than memorizing patterns.
________________________________________
39. Cache access patterns and ownership
The entire lesson can be reduced to this:
Cache-Aside
Application owns cache + DB coordination

Read-Through
Cache owns read loading

Write-Through
Cache owns synchronous persistence

Write-Back
Cache owns asynchronous persistence

Refresh-Ahead
Cache proactively refreshes before expiry
That's the conceptual foundation.
________________________________________
40. Where these patterns fit in a real Java system
A common Cache-Aside implementation could look like:
public User getUser(long id) {

    String key = "user:" + id;

    User cached = redis.get(key);

    if (cached != null) {
        return cached;
    }

    User user = userRepository.findById(id);

    redis.set(key, user);

    return user;
}
Write:
public void updateUser(User user) {

    userRepository.update(user);

    redis.delete("user:" + user.id());
}
This is intentionally simple.
Production code must additionally think about:
serialization
TTL
null values
concurrent misses
timeouts
Redis failure
DB failure
stale values
versioning
metrics
Those are the subjects coming next.
