22. Java implementation — now we make it concrete
Suppose you have:
public User getUser(String id) {
    return userClient.getUser(id);
}
A naive retry:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    try {
        return userClient.getUser(id);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // retry
    }
}

throw new UserServiceException();
This code is dangerous.
Why?
Because it has no idea:
•	what failed 
•	whether the error is transient 
•	whether the operation is idempotent 
•	whether the request has enough time left 
•	how long to wait 
•	whether other clients are retrying 
•	whether the dependency is overloaded 
•	whether another layer is already retrying 
It is syntactically correct.
It is architecturally immature.
________________________________________
23. A more realistic mental model
Think of retry as:
execute attempt
      |
      v
  outcome?
      |
      +---- success ------------------> return
      |
      +---- permanent failure --------> fail
      |
      +---- transient failure
                    |
                    v
             retry allowed?
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
         no                  yes
          |                   |
        fail             remaining budget?
                              |
                       +------+------+
                       |             |
                      no            yes
                       |             |
                     fail      remaining deadline?
                                     |
                              +------+------+
                              |             |
                             no            yes
                              |             |
