16. Production Redis Architecture

The first thing to understand is this:
Redis is fundamentally an in-memory data structure server driven by an event loop.
A simplified mental model is:
                 ┌──────────────────────┐
                 │      Redis Client    │
                 │ Java / Python / Go   │
                 └──────────┬───────────┘
                            │ TCP
                            ▼
                 ┌──────────────────────┐
                 │    Redis Server      │
                 │                      │
                 │  Network I/O         │
                 │       ↓              │
                 │   Event Loop         │
                 │       ↓              │
                 │ Command Processing   │
                 │       ↓              │
                 │ Data Structures      │
                 │                      │
                 │ RAM                  │
                 └──────────────────────┘
But this diagram hides a lot of important details.
Let's build the real mental model.
________________________________________
1. Why was Redis designed this way?
Suppose we build a traditional server.
A client sends:
GET user:101
The server needs to:
1.	receive bytes from the network 
2.	parse the command 
3.	find the key 
4.	access the data structure 
5.	produce the response 
6.	write response bytes to the network 
A traditional architecture might create a thread for every connection:
Client A ──> Thread A
Client B ──> Thread B
Client C ──> Thread C
Client D ──> Thread D
But imagine:
100,000 connections
Creating and managing 100,000 threads is expensive.
Threads require:
•	stack memory 
•	scheduling 
•	context switching 
•	synchronization 
•	locks 
•	coordination 
Redis took a different approach.
Instead of:
Connection
    ↓
Thread
    ↓
Command
the core idea became:
Many connections
       ↓
 I/O Multiplexing
       ↓
   Event Loop
       ↓
 Command execution
This is one of the most important Redis concepts.
________________________________________
2. What does "single-threaded Redis" actually mean?
You've probably heard:
"Redis is single-threaded."
This statement is historically useful but incomplete for modern Redis.
The important distinction is:
Command execution
Traditionally, Redis executes commands sequentially on the main thread.
Conceptually:
Command A
   ↓
execute completely

Command B
   ↓
execute completely

Command C
   ↓
execute completely
So if:
Client A → SET x 10
Client B → GET x
Redis does not have two threads simultaneously modifying the same data structure in the traditional command-processing model.
This gives Redis a very powerful property:
A large portion of Redis command execution doesn't need traditional locking between client commands.
Compare:
Multi-threaded server

Thread A ─┐
          ├── shared data ── locks/CAS/etc.
Thread B ─┘
with:
Redis command execution

Command A
    ↓
   RAM
    ↑
Command B
Sequential execution dramatically simplifies concurrency.
________________________________________
3. But how can one thread handle thousands of clients?
This is where I/O multiplexing comes in.
Imagine 10,000 clients.
Most of them aren't constantly sending commands.
At a particular moment:
Client 1  waiting
Client 2  waiting
Client 3  has data
Client 4  waiting
Client 5  waiting
...
Client 9000 waiting
It would be wasteful for Redis to have:
10,000 threads
Instead, the operating system provides mechanisms such as:
•	epoll on Linux 
•	kqueue on BSD/macOS 
•	similar event notification mechanisms on other platforms 
Redis can ask the OS:
"Tell me which sockets are ready for I/O."
The OS might respond:
Socket 17 → readable
Socket 42 → readable
Socket 91 → writable
Redis then processes those ready events.
Conceptually:
              OS Kernel
                  │
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          │                │
       Socket 17        Socket 42
       readable         readable
          │                │
          └───────┬────────┘
                  ↓
             Event Loop
                  ↓
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
   Client A   Client B   Client C
       ↓          ↓          ↓
    command    command    command
This is I/O multiplexing.
________________________________________
4. Event Loop — the heart of Redis
At a conceptual level, the event loop looks like:
while (server is running) {

    wait for events

    process readable sockets

    process commands

    process writable sockets

    execute scheduled/background work
}
Don't take this as literal Redis source code. It's the conceptual model.
The critical idea is:
WAIT
 ↓
EVENT ARRIVES
 ↓
PROCESS
 ↓
WAIT
 ↓
EVENT ARRIVES
 ↓
PROCESS
This is very different from:
Thread 1 → Client 1
Thread 2 → Client 2
Thread 3 → Client 3
...
________________________________________
5. Let's follow one real Redis request
Suppose your Java application executes:
redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("user:101");
At the application level you think:
Java
 ↓
Redis GET
 ↓
result
Internally, much more happens.
Step 1 — Java client creates/sends bytes
Your Redis client converts the logical command:
GET user:101
into the Redis protocol representation.
Historically this is RESP, with modern Redis supporting newer RESP versions as well.
Those bytes travel through:
Java Application
      ↓
TCP socket
      ↓
Operating System
      ↓
Network
      ↓
Redis machine
________________________________________
6. Redis receives network data
The Redis server has a listening socket.
For example conceptually:
Redis
  |
  +--- listening socket
          |
          +--- Client A connection
          +--- Client B connection
          +--- Client C connection
The OS tells Redis:
Client B socket is readable.
The event loop handles it.
________________________________________
7. Redis reads the request
Redis reads bytes from the socket.
For example:
GET user:101
The bytes are accumulated into the client's input buffer.
Conceptually:
Client connection
       ↓
Input buffer
       ↓
"GET user:101"
Then Redis parses the protocol.
________________________________________
8. Command lookup
Redis identifies:
GET
as a Redis command.
Conceptually:
"GET"
  ↓
command table
  ↓
GET command implementation
You can think of the command table as something like:
SET  → SET implementation
GET  → GET implementation
DEL  → DEL implementation
HSET → HSET implementation
LPUSH → LPUSH implementation
...
The command implementation then operates on Redis's internal data structures.
We'll study those deeply in Topic 02 — Redis Object Model + Memory.
________________________________________
9. Redis finds the key
Suppose:
GET user:101
Redis needs to locate:
user:101
Conceptually:
GET
 ↓
key lookup
 ↓
dictionary/hash table
 ↓
user:101
 ↓
Redis object
 ↓
value
For example:
user:101
   ↓
String object
   ↓
"{name:Gopal, ...}"
Don't worry about the exact internal representation yet.
That's our next major topic.
________________________________________
10. The response is generated
Suppose the value is:
hello
Redis generates the appropriate RESP response.
Conceptually:
GET user:101
      ↓
lookup
      ↓
"hello"
      ↓
RESP encoding
      ↓
output buffer
So now:
Redis
  ↓
client output buffer
  ↓
TCP socket
  ↓
Java client
The Java Redis client receives the response and converts it into the Java value.
________________________________________
11. The important performance insight
Look at what Redis didn't do.
For a simple:
GET key
it didn't need:
disk seek
database transaction
database connection pool
row lock
SQL parser
query optimizer
network call to another DB
Instead:
Network
   ↓
Parse
   ↓
Memory lookup
   ↓
Response
That's why Redis can achieve extremely low latency.
________________________________________
12. Why memory matters so much
Consider a disk-based database.
Even with modern SSDs/NVMe, accessing persistent storage involves substantially more work than accessing RAM.
Redis's primary dataset is held in memory.
Conceptually:
                 Redis
                   │
                   ▼
                RAM
          ┌───────────────┐
          │ key → value   │
          │ key → value   │
          │ key → value   │
          └───────────────┘
So:
GET
 ↓
RAM lookup
 ↓
response
This is one of the fundamental reasons Redis is fast.
But this immediately introduces a major problem:
RAM is limited and volatile.
And that leads directly to:
•	eviction 
•	persistence 
•	replication 
•	failover 
•	memory fragmentation 
which we'll study later.
________________________________________
13. Why doesn't Redis simply use multiple threads for commands?
This is a very important system-design question.
Suppose two threads execute:
Thread A:
INCR counter
and
Thread B:
INCR counter
Now you need to ensure the operation is correct.
You potentially need:
locks
CAS
atomic operations
synchronization
memory ordering
Redis's traditional approach is simpler:
Thread/Main Event Loop

INCR counter
     ↓
complete

INCR counter
     ↓
complete
Therefore commands can appear atomic from the perspective of other commands.
This gives us an extremely useful property:
A Redis command is generally executed as an indivisible unit with respect to other commands.
For example:
SET A 10
doesn't get half-executed while another client reads the intermediate state.
________________________________________
14. But there is a HUGE catch
Single-threaded command execution does not mean:
"Redis can never be blocked."
This is one of the most important things to understand.
Consider:
GET user:1
Very cheap.
Maybe:
O(1)
Now imagine a command that has to process millions of elements.
Conceptually:
Command
   ↓
process 100
process 1,000
process 10,000
process 100,000
process 1,000,000
While that command is executing:
Event Loop
     ↓
BUSY
Other commands may have to wait.
For example:
Client A:
BIG expensive operation
        ↓
   Event Loop
        ↓
████████████████████

Client B:
GET user:101
        ↓
       WAIT
This is one of the most important Redis operational concepts:
A slow command can block the Redis event loop and therefore increase latency for unrelated clients.
________________________________________
15. Example: the famous latency problem
Imagine:
Client A → expensive command
Client B → GET
Client C → SET
Client D → INCR
Execution:
        Event Loop

A ──────► expensive command
          │
          │
          │
          ▼
       finishes

B ──────► GET
C ──────► SET
D ──────► INCR
So even though:
GET
normally takes microseconds-level processing time, the user might observe:
GET latency = 500 ms
because it was waiting behind another operation.
This is why Redis performance engineering isn't simply:
"Redis is fast."
You must understand:
What is occupying the event loop?
________________________________________
16. Does modern Redis still have only one thread?
This is where your 6+ years experience matters.
The statement:
"Redis is single-threaded."
should not be used as a complete description of modern Redis.
Modern Redis versions use additional threads for certain work, and Redis has introduced threaded I/O and other background processing improvements.
But the key conceptual distinction remains:
                Redis
                  │
       ┌──────────┴───────────┐
       │                      │
 Main command execution   Background/
 event loop               auxiliary work
       │
       ▼
 Data structure operations
The main command-processing path has historically been designed around sequential execution, while some I/O and background tasks can use other threads.
This distinction becomes especially important when discussing:
•	Redis 6+ 
•	threaded I/O 
•	lazy freeing 
•	persistence 
•	replication 
•	cluster behavior 
We'll revisit these when appropriate.
________________________________________
17. Event Loop vs Java Thread Pool
As a Java developer, compare Redis with a typical Spring Boot application.
Typical Java server
You might have:
HTTP Requests
      ↓
Tomcat
      ↓
Thread Pool
      ↓
Controller
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Repository
Potentially:
Request 1 → Thread 1
Request 2 → Thread 2
Request 3 → Thread 3
Request 4 → Thread 4
Redis's conceptual model is closer to:
Connections
      ↓
I/O Multiplexing
      ↓
Event Loop
      ↓
Commands
      ↓
Sequential execution
The concurrency model is fundamentally different.
________________________________________
18. Event loop does NOT mean "no concurrency"
This is another common misconception.
Redis can have:
10,000 connected clients
while using an event-driven model.
The clients are concurrent from the network perspective.
But command processing can be serialized through the main execution path.
Think:
          10,000 clients
                │
                ▼
       ┌────────────────┐
       │ I/O Multiplexer│
       └───────┬────────┘
               │
               ▼
          Event Loop
               │
       ┌───────┴───────┐
       ▼       ▼       ▼
      GET     SET     INCR
       │       │       │
       └───────┼───────┘
               ▼
              RAM
Concurrency is handled primarily by efficient I/O multiplexing, rather than by creating one execution thread per connection.
________________________________________
19. A very important distinction: connection vs request
Suppose you have:
1000 Java application threads
but your application uses a Redis connection pool of:
50 Redis connections
Redis doesn't see:
1000 Java threads
It sees approximately:
50 TCP connections
Those connections can send many commands over time.
Modern Redis clients commonly use connection pooling and/or multiplexing strategies depending on the client and API.
This is why understanding:
TCP connection
      ↓
Redis connection
      ↓
command
      ↓
response
is important before we go deeper into Redis.
________________________________________
20. What actually consumes Redis CPU?
For a simple command:
GET key
CPU work includes things like:
socket processing
     ↓
protocol parsing
     ↓
command lookup
     ↓
key lookup
     ↓
data structure operation
     ↓
response construction
     ↓
socket writing
Notice:
The actual data lookup may be only a small fraction of the total work.
This becomes relevant when optimizing Redis.
If Redis CPU is high, don't immediately assume:
"HashMap lookup is slow."
You need to investigate:
commands/sec
command complexity
serialization
network I/O
protocol parsing
large values
large collections
expiration work
persistence
replication
background work
________________________________________
21. Redis architecture — first-level mental model
Now build this model in your head:
                         CLIENTS
                            │
                    TCP Connections
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │    Redis Server     │
                 │                     │
                 │ Network I/O         │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ I/O Multiplexing    │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ Event Loop          │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ Command Parser      │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ Command Execution   │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ Redis Objects       │
                 │       ↓             │
                 │ In-Memory Dataset   │
                 └─────────────────────┘
Then around it:
                       Redis
                         │
        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
        │                │                │
     RDB/AOF         Replication       Cluster
        │                │                │
        ▼                ▼                ▼
    Persistence       Replicas        Sharding
We'll eventually build all of that.
________________________________________
22. The most important architectural trade-off
Redis's design gives us:
Advantage
Sequential command execution
        +
In-memory data
        +
Efficient event-driven I/O
→ extremely low latency and high throughput.
But the trade-off is
Expensive command
      ↓
blocks execution path
      ↓
other commands wait
      ↓
latency increases
Therefore:
Redis performance is not just about how fast one command is. It's about how commands interact with the single command-execution path.
This is a much more valuable understanding than memorizing "Redis is single-threaded."
________________________________________
23. What you should be able to explain now
Before moving to Topic 02, you should be able to answer these yourself:
Q1. Why doesn't Redis create one thread per client?
Because thousands of client connections can be handled efficiently using non-blocking I/O + OS-level I/O multiplexing, avoiding the memory and scheduling overhead of one thread per connection.
Q2. What is the Event Loop?
A continuously running event-driven mechanism that waits for I/O events and processes ready connections/commands.
Q3. Why can Redis be extremely fast?
Primarily because:
in-memory data
+
efficient event-driven networking
+
low overhead
+
sequential command execution
Q4. What is the danger of sequential command execution?
A sufficiently expensive/blocking operation can delay unrelated commands.
Q5. Does "single-threaded Redis" mean the entire Redis process has exactly one thread?
No. Modern Redis uses additional threads for certain I/O and background tasks. The important historical/current architectural concept is the main command execution path and its event-driven nature.
Q6. Why doesn't Redis need a lock around every command?
Because commands on the main execution path are processed sequentially rather than concurrently modifying the same data structures.
________________________________________
24. One level deeper: the mental model I want you to keep
Don't remember:
"Redis is single-threaded."
Remember this instead:
                 REDIS

        Many concurrent clients
                  │
