And the golden rule:
If the main command execution path is busy, everyone waiting behind it can experience latency.
That single idea will help you understand Redis performance, failure modes, latency spikes, and production

02. Redis Object Model + Memory
This is one of the most important Redis topics because Redis is fundamentally an in-memory data structure engine.
If you understand only:
Redis stores data in RAM
you know almost nothing about Redis memory.
As a 6+ year Java developer, I want you to think about Redis memory similarly to how you think about:
Java Object
    ↓
object header
    ↓
fields
    ↓
references
    ↓
heap allocation
    ↓
GC
    ↓
memory fragmentation
Redis has its own internal object representation, encodings, allocators, pointers, metadata, and fragmentation behavior.
________________________________________
1. Start with the fundamental Redis model
When you execute:
SET user:101 "Gopal"
you should NOT mentally model Redis as:
Map<String, String>
That is too simplistic.
A better model is:
Redis database
    ↓
Dictionary
    ↓
key
    ↓
Redis object
    ↓
value object
Conceptually:
DB
 |
 +-- Dictionary
       |
       +-- "user:101"
              |
              +-- Redis Object
                     |
                     +-- type = STRING
                     +-- encoding = ...
                     +-- pointer = ...
The actual implementation has more details, but this is the mental model we need.
________________________________________
2. Redis database is fundamentally a dictionary
At a high level, Redis needs a structure that maps:
key → value
For example:
user:101 → "Gopal"
user:102 → "Krishna"
cart:501 → {...}
counter → 100
Conceptually:
Dictionary

Hash Table
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ bucket 0                     │
│ bucket 1 → user:101          │
│ bucket 2                     │
│ bucket 3 → counter           │
│ bucket 4 → cart:501          │
└──────────────────────────────┘
This is conceptually similar to:
Map<String, Object>
but Redis's internal implementation is much more specialized.
________________________________________
3. Redis has the concept of an object
A Redis value isn't simply:
char*
Redis uses an internal object abstraction historically represented by redisObject.
Conceptually, think of it as something like:
Redis Object
-------------------------
type
encoding
refcount
pointer
-------------------------
The exact structure and implementation details vary across Redis versions, so don't memorize a specific C struct layout as universal.
But these concepts are extremely important.
________________________________________
4. Type
The object has a logical Redis type.
Examples:
STRING
LIST
SET
HASH
ZSET
STREAM
...
For:
SET name Gopal
the value is logically:
STRING
For:
LPUSH users Gopal
the value is:
LIST
For:
SADD developers Java
the value is:
SET
So:
Redis Object
      |
      +── type
answers:
"What kind of Redis data structure is this?"
________________________________________
5. Type vs encoding
This distinction is extremely important.
Redis has:
TYPE
and:
ENCODING
They are not the same thing.
For example:
TYPE = STRING
doesn't tell you exactly how Redis stores that string internally.
Redis can use different internal representations depending on the data.
Think:
Logical Type
     ↓
STRING
     ↓
Possible internal representation
     ↓
different encoding/storage strategy
Similarly:
Logical Type
     ↓
LIST
     ↓
Internal encoding
This separation allows Redis to optimize memory and operations.
________________________________________
6. Why does Redis need different encodings?
Imagine two strings:
counter = "100"
and:
description = "This is a very large description..."
They are both:
STRING
but their optimal internal representations may be different.
Similarly, consider a Set containing:
{1, 2, 3}
versus:
{1000000, "Java", "Spring", "Kafka", ...}
Using the exact same internal structure for every case would waste memory.
Therefore Redis can choose representations based on:
•	value characteristics 
•	size 
•	number of elements 
•	element types 
•	operation requirements 
This is one of the key reasons Redis can be memory efficient.
________________________________________
7. Redis object vs actual data structure
This distinction is critical.
Suppose:
SET counter 100
You have:
Key
 ↓
"counter"
and:
Value
 ↓
Redis Object
 ↓
STRING
 ↓
internal representation
For a Hash:
HSET user:101 name Gopal age 30
you conceptually have:
Key
 ↓
"user:101"
 ↓
Redis Object
 ↓
HASH
 ↓
internal hash representation
 ↓
fields + values
So the Redis object is a wrapper/metadata layer around the underlying value representation.
________________________________________
8. Refcount — why is this needed?
Redis objects historically use reference counting as part of their memory management.
Conceptually:
Redis Object
    |
    +-- refcount = N
If:
refcount = 1
one reference owns it.
If another part of the system references the object:
refcount = 2
When a reference disappears:
refcount--
When it reaches zero:
free object
This is very different from Java.
Java:
Object
  ↓
GC roots
  ↓
reachability
  ↓
Garbage Collector
Redis has historically relied heavily on:
reference counting
for object lifetime management, with additional memory-management mechanisms for other allocations.
________________________________________
9. Why reference counting is useful in Redis
Imagine:
Object A
  ↑
  |
Reference
If nobody references it anymore:
refcount = 0
Redis can release it immediately.
This can provide deterministic object lifetime.
But reference counting has a classic problem:
Cycles.
For example:
A → B
B → A
If both have references to each other, simple reference counting can't determine that they're unreachable.
Redis's data model and object management aren't simply a generic cyclic object graph like a Java heap, so this problem doesn't map directly onto normal Redis key/value usage.
The bigger point for you is:
Java → tracing GC
Redis → reference counting + allocator/object lifecycle mechanisms
________________________________________
10. The key itself consumes memory
This is a common production mistake.
Suppose you store:
user:1000001 → "Gopal"
You might think:
"Gopal" = 5 bytes
Therefore:
5 bytes
Wrong.
Redis needs memory for:
key
+
key metadata
+
value object
+
value representation
+
dictionary entry
+
allocator overhead
+
alignment
+
potential fragmentation
Conceptually:
Total Memory
=
Key Memory
+
Value Memory
+
Object Metadata
+
Dictionary Overhead
+
Allocator Overhead
+
Fragmentation
+
Other Redis Overhead
This is one of the most important Redis memory formulas.
________________________________________
11. Compare with Java HashMap
As a Java developer, this comparison is useful.
Suppose:
Map<String, String> map;
You store:
map.put("user:101", "Gopal");
You don't pay only:
"user:101"
+
"Gopal"
You also have:
HashMap table
Node
hash
references
String object
String backing representation
object headers
alignment
allocator/JVM heap overhead
Redis has a similar fundamental problem.
If you store:
SET a b
the memory consumption is significantly larger than:
1 byte key + 1 byte value
because data structures have structural overhead.
________________________________________
12. Redis uses SDS for strings
One of the most important internal structures is:
SDS — Simple Dynamic String
Redis historically does not simply use raw C strings everywhere.
Why?
Because normal C strings have a major limitation:
char*
uses:
'\0'
as the terminator.
Finding the length requires scanning:
O(N)
Conceptually:
strlen("hello")
may scan the characters.
Redis wants string operations to be efficient and safer.
SDS stores length metadata.
Conceptually:
SDS

┌─────────┬─────────┬───────────────┐
│ length  │ free    │    buffer     │
└─────────┴─────────┴───────────────┘
So Redis can know:
length = 5
without scanning the entire string.
________________________________________
13. Why free exists
Suppose the string currently contains:
hello
Redis may have allocated some unused capacity.
Conceptually:
length = 5
free   = 10

[hello..........]
Then appending data doesn't necessarily require reallocating immediately.
For example:
hello
becomes:
hello world
If enough free capacity exists:
[hello world.....]
Redis can reuse the existing allocation.
This is a standard dynamic-array optimization.
________________________________________
14. Why SDS is important
SDS gives Redis several advantages:
O(1) length retrieval
Instead of:
strlen()
scanning the string:
length
is already available.
Binary safety
Redis strings can contain arbitrary binary data.
They're not conceptually limited to:
text ending with '\0'
Efficient appending
Unused capacity can reduce reallocations.
Better memory management
SDS can choose different header sizes depending on string length.
This is another memory optimization.
________________________________________
15. Redis doesn't use one SDS layout for every string
This is another subtle point.
Redis has historically used multiple SDS header variants depending on length.
Conceptually:
Tiny string
    ↓
small metadata header

Medium string
    ↓
larger header

Large string
    ↓
larger metadata
Why?
Because if you store millions of tiny strings, even a few bytes of unnecessary metadata become huge.
Imagine:
10 million keys
and you waste:
8 bytes per object
That's:
80 MB
just from a seemingly tiny overhead.
At hundreds of millions of objects, these small differences become gigabytes.
________________________________________
16. Integer encoding optimization
Redis can optimize certain string values that are integers.
For example:
SET counter 100
Internally, Redis may represent suitable integer values more efficiently than storing the full textual representation in the same way as an arbitrary string.
Conceptually:
"100"
can potentially be represented as:
integer = 100
rather than:
ASCII characters
'1'
'0'
'0'
This is an example of:
Logical type ≠ physical representation.
Again:
TYPE = STRING
doesn't necessarily mean:
raw character array
________________________________________
17. Shared objects
Redis can also use sharing/interning-like optimizations for certain frequently used values.
For example, small integers are particularly suitable candidates.
Conceptually:
Client A → 1
Client B → 1
Client C → 1
Client D → 1
Instead of unnecessarily creating completely independent representations:
1
1
1
1
Redis can reuse certain objects.
Conceptually:
             ┌── Client A
             │
Shared "1" ──┼── Client B
             │
             ├── Client C
             │
             └── Client D
Reference counting becomes relevant here:
refcount++
This reduces memory usage.
The exact set of shared objects and implementation behavior depends on Redis version/configuration, so don't memorize "all integers are always shared."
________________________________________
18. The Redis dictionary
Now let's look at the key space.
At a high level:
Redis DB
   ↓
dict
   ↓
hash table
The dictionary maps:
key → value object
Conceptually:
bucket[0] → entry
bucket[1] → entry → entry
bucket[2]
bucket[3] → entry
Each dictionary entry itself consumes memory.
So if you have:
100 million keys
the dictionary overhead alone becomes substantial.
This is why Redis memory planning cannot simply be:
sum(value sizes)
________________________________________
19. Hash table collisions
Just like Java's HashMap, Redis's dictionary can have hash collisions.
Conceptually:
hash(key)
    ↓
bucket index
Two keys can map to the same bucket:
key A ──┐
        ├── bucket 10
key B ──┘
Redis needs a collision-resolution mechanism.
The important conceptual point is:
Key
 ↓
Hash function
 ↓
Bucket
 ↓
Dictionary entry
This should feel familiar from your Java HashMap studies.
________________________________________
20. Redis dictionary resizing
Another important internal concept:
Hash tables need to resize as the number of elements changes.
Conceptually:
Small table

[ ][ ][ ][ ]
becomes:
Larger table

[ ][ ][ ][ ][ ][ ][ ][ ]
Redis uses mechanisms to resize its dictionaries efficiently.
Why can't it just stop the world and move millions of entries instantly?
Because Redis is latency-sensitive.
Imagine:
100 million entries
and Redis performs a giant synchronous resize.
The event loop could be blocked for a significant amount of time.
Therefore Redis uses techniques such as incremental rehashing.
________________________________________
21. Incremental rehashing
This is a very important concept.
Instead of:
Move 100 million entries
    ↓
then continue
Redis can conceptually do:
Operation 1
 ↓
move some entries

Operation 2
 ↓
move some entries

Operation 3
 ↓
move some entries
So the migration is spread across time.
Conceptually:
Old Hash Table             New Hash Table

bucket 0 ────────────────► bucket 0
bucket 1 ────────────────► bucket 1
bucket 2
bucket 3 ────────────────► bucket 3
...
This reduces latency spikes.
________________________________________
22. Why this matters in production
Imagine:
Redis
10 million keys
Then traffic causes rapid growth:
10M
 ↓
20M
 ↓
50M
The internal dictionary needs to adapt.
If resizing were always a giant blocking operation, you'd see:
Normal latency:

0.5 ms
0.4 ms
0.6 ms
0.5 ms

Resize:

████████████████ 200 ms

Then:

0.5 ms
0.4 ms
Incremental techniques help avoid large pauses.
This is exactly the type of implementation detail that separates:
"I know Redis"
from:
"I understand why Redis behaves the way it does."
________________________________________
23. Memory allocator — Redis doesn't directly manage every byte
Redis needs a memory allocator.
On many standard Redis builds, jemalloc is commonly used.
Conceptually:
Redis
  ↓
malloc/free abstraction
  ↓
allocator
  ↓
OS memory
Why does this matter?
Because:
requested memory
and:
actual process memory
are not necessarily equal.
This leads us to one of the biggest Redis memory concepts:
Memory fragmentation.
________________________________________
24. Memory fragmentation
Suppose Redis allocates:
100 bytes
then frees:
40 bytes
then allocates:
30 bytes
The memory may become fragmented.
Conceptually:
Memory:

[Used][Free][Used][Free][Used][Free]
Even if enough total free memory exists, it may not be arranged optimally for future allocations.
So:
Logical data size
≠
Allocated memory
≠
Resident physical memory
This distinction is critical.
________________________________________
25. Three different memory numbers
When diagnosing Redis memory, think about at least three levels.
1. Dataset memory
Memory required for actual Redis data structures.
2. Allocated memory
Memory obtained from the allocator.
3. RSS
Resident Set Size — memory pages currently resident in physical memory.
Conceptually:
Dataset
   ↓
Allocator overhead
   ↓
Fragmentation
   ↓
RSS
Therefore:
used_memory
and:
used_memory_rss
can differ significantly.
________________________________________
26. Example
Suppose:
used_memory = 8 GB
but:
used_memory_rss = 11 GB
You might have approximately:
3 GB
of additional process-resident memory due to allocator behavior, fragmentation, other allocations, and related overhead.
Don't immediately conclude:
"Redis is leaking 3 GB."
You need to understand allocator fragmentation and the rest of Redis's memory footprint.
________________________________________
27. Fragmentation ratio
A commonly useful metric is conceptually:
mem_fragmentation_ratio
Historically, it is roughly related to:
RSS / used_memory
So if:
RSS = 12 GB
used_memory = 8 GB
ratio:
12 / 8 = 1.5
A high ratio can indicate significant fragmentation, although you should not diagnose fragmentation from this ratio alone.
Modern Redis versions expose more detailed memory statistics, and the exact interpretation depends on allocator/platform behavior.
________________________________________
28. Why fragmentation becomes a production problem
Suppose:
Physical RAM = 16 GB
and Redis reports:
used_memory = 12 GB
RSS = 15 GB
You may think:
12 GB data
but the OS sees approximately:
15 GB resident
Now you only have:
1 GB
of comfortable headroom before serious memory pressure.
If Redis continues growing:
RSS → 16 GB+
the operating system may start reclaiming memory, swapping under some configurations, or the process may face OOM conditions depending on environment.
For Redis, this can be disastrous for latency.
________________________________________
29. Why swapping Redis is dangerous
Redis is designed around memory-speed access.
If hot Redis pages get swapped to disk:
Redis GET
 ↓
memory page not resident
 ↓
page fault
 ↓
disk I/O
 ↓
huge latency
You go from:
microseconds-ish
toward:
milliseconds or much worse
depending on the storage and workload.
Therefore production Redis deployments generally need careful OS memory configuration and sufficient RAM headroom.
________________________________________
30. Memory overhead is especially painful for tiny keys
Consider:
SET a 1
SET b 2
SET c 3
The actual logical payload is tiny:
a = 1
b = 2
c = 3
But every entry has structural overhead.
Conceptually:
Key
+
SDS metadata
+
Dictionary entry
+
Redis object
+
Value representation
+
Allocator overhead
So tiny values can have a surprisingly high:
overhead / payload
ratio.
This is why Redis is often much more memory-efficient when data is structured appropriately rather than represented as millions of tiny independent objects.
________________________________________
31. Example: many keys vs one Hash
Suppose you need:
user:101:name
user:101:age
user:101:city
user:101:email
Approach A:
SET user:101:name Gopal
SET user:101:age 30
SET user:101:city Hyderabad
SET user:101:email ...
You have multiple independent top-level keys.
Approach B:
HSET user:101 name Gopal age 30 city Hyderabad email ...
Now you have:
one top-level key
      ↓
one Redis HASH
      ↓
multiple fields
Depending on the data and Redis version/encoding, the second approach can significantly reduce overhead.
But don't conclude "Hash is always more memory efficient."
We'll study exactly when this is true when we reach:
Strings, Lists, Sets, Hashes, Sorted Sets.
________________________________________
32. Redis memory has several layers
You should now have this mental model:
                 Redis Memory
                      │
        ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
        │             │              │
      Keys          Values       Metadata
        │             │              │
      SDS         Objects       Dictionary
                    │
                    ▼
                Encodings
                    │
                    ▼
                Allocator
                    │
                    ▼
             Fragmentation
                    │
                    ▼
                   RSS
This is much closer to the real system.
________________________________________
33. The Java comparison
This comparison is worth remembering.
Java	Redis
Object	Redis object
String	SDS-based string representation
HashMap	Dictionary/hash table
Object header	Object metadata
Heap	Process memory
GC	Redis object/allocator lifecycle mechanisms
