Heap fragmentation	Allocator fragmentation
Reference graph	Redis internal object references
Primitive optimization	Specialized Redis encodings
Don't take these as exact one-to-one mappings.
They're mental bridges, not implementation equivalences.
________________________________________
34. A subtle but important distinction: logical memory vs physical memory
Suppose you run:
SET user:101 Gopal
Logically:
key = 8 bytes-ish
value = 5 bytes-ish
But physically:
key representation
+
value representation
+
Redis metadata
+
dictionary entry
+
allocator metadata
+
alignment
+
fragmentation
So:
5-byte value
does NOT mean:
5 bytes of RAM
This is one of the most common mistakes when estimating Redis capacity.
________________________________________
35. Why Redis memory knowledge matters for system design
Suppose an interviewer/system-design scenario says:
"We need to cache 500 million users in Redis."
A weak answer:
"500 million × average user size."
A stronger answer:
Raw payload
+
key overhead
+
object overhead
+
data structure overhead
+
allocator overhead
+
fragmentation
+
replication overhead
+
failover capacity
+
operational headroom
And if you have replicas:
Primary
   +
Replica 1
   +
Replica 2
your effective memory requirement can multiply.
We'll get to replication later.
