3.6 SETNX — String + conditional creation
Another extremely important command:
SETNX lock:order:101 1
Meaning:
SET if key does NOT exist
If the key doesn't exist:
1
If it already exists:
0
This concept is useful for:
Distributed locks
Idempotency
Deduplication
Leader election patterns
Modern Redis applications commonly use the atomic SET form with conditions and expiry, for example:
SET lock:order:101 token NX EX 30
Conceptually:
SET
 |
 +-- NX → only if key doesn't exist
 |
 +-- EX 30 → expire after 30 seconds
We'll study distributed locking properly later.
________________________________________
3.7 GETSET / GETDEL style atomic operations
Redis also provides commands that combine retrieval and mutation.
For example:
GETDEL key
Conceptually:
GET value
+
DELETE key
as one Redis command.
This is another example of an important Redis principle:
Prefer a single Redis command when the operation can express your required atomic behavior.
Instead of application-side:
GET
↓
application
↓
DEL
you can use a server-side atomic command where appropriate.
