61. Interview-Level Questions You Should Be Able to Answer
Q1. Does Redis immediately delete a key when TTL reaches zero?
Not necessarily.
The key becomes expired, but physical deletion can happen through lazy expiration when accessed or active expiration work.
________________________________________
Q2. Why can an expired Redis key still consume memory?
Because expiration and physical deletion are separate events. Until lazy or active expiration removes the key, its memory can remain allocated.
________________________________________
Q3. Why doesn't Redis use one timer per key?
That would be too expensive at large scale. Redis uses expiration metadata plus lazy and active expiration mechanisms.
________________________________________
Q4. What is the difference between expiration and eviction?
Expiration → time-based
Eviction    → memory-pressure-based
________________________________________
Q5. Difference between LRU and LFU?
LRU → recent usage
LFU → usage frequency
________________________________________
Q6. Difference between allkeys-lru and volatile-lru?
allkeys-lru
    ↓
all keys are candidates

volatile-lru
    ↓
only keys with TTL are candidates
________________________________________
Q7. Why is Redis LRU approximate?
Exact global LRU tracking would have significant CPU and memory overhead. Redis samples candidates and chooses a good approximation.
________________________________________
Q8. Why can LFU be better than LRU?
For workloads where frequently popular objects should remain cached even if they haven't been accessed very recently.
________________________________________
Q9. What is cache stampede?
Many requests simultaneously miss the same cache key after expiration and hit the backing database/service at once.
________________________________________
