Now we move from “how Redis manages memory” to:
How does Redis save its in-memory data to disk?
This is important because Redis is primarily an in-memory database.
If Redis stores:
RAM
│
├── user:101 → Gopal
├── user:102 → John
├── cart:101 → ...
└── session:xyz → ...
and the Redis process crashes, normally the RAM contents disappear.
So Redis provides persistence mechanisms.
The first one we learn is:
RDB — Redis Database Backup
RDB stands for Redis Database Backup.
The basic idea is:
Redis RAM
    │
    │ snapshot
    ↓
RDB file on disk
Instead of recording every individual operation, Redis periodically creates a snapshot of the dataset.
________________________________________
1. Why does Redis need persistence?
Imagine:
Application
     ↓
   Redis
     ↓
    RAM
Redis is extremely fast because data is primarily held in memory.
But RAM is volatile.
If the Redis process disappears:
Redis process crashes
        ↓
RAM contents disappear
Without persistence:
user:101 → Gopal
user:102 → John
user:103 → Alice
could be lost.
Persistence gives us:
RAM
 ↓
Disk
 ↓
survive restart
________________________________________
2. Redis persistence options
At a high level, Redis has two major persistence mechanisms:
Persistence
    │
    ├── RDB
    │
    └── AOF
We are focusing on:
RDB
We'll study AOF separately.
________________________________________
3. RDB mental model
Think of RDB as a photograph.
Suppose Redis currently contains:
user:101 → Gopal
user:102 → John
user:103 → Alice
Redis creates a snapshot:
             Redis RAM
                 │
                 │ snapshot
                 ↓
          ┌──────────────┐
          │ dump.rdb     │
          │              │
          │ user:101     │
          │ user:102     │
          │ user:103     │
          └──────────────┘
The RDB file represents the dataset at approximately a particular point in time.
That's why RDB is called:
Point-in-time snapshot persistence
________________________________________
4. RDB is not a transaction log
This distinction is very important.
Suppose:
SET A 10
SET B 20
SET C 30
AOF conceptually records operations:
SET A 10
SET B 20
SET C 30
RDB instead produces a snapshot:
A → 10
B → 20
C → 30
So:
RDB
 ↓
snapshot of state


AOF
 ↓
log of operations
We'll later see why this creates different durability and recovery characteristics.
________________________________________
5. How does RDB snapshotting happen?
There are two important commands:
SAVE
and:
BGSAVE
Understanding their difference is extremely important.
________________________________________
6. SAVE
SAVE tells Redis:
Create an RDB snapshot synchronously.
Conceptually:
Redis process
     │
     │ SAVE
     ↓
create RDB
     │
     ↓
finish
     │
     ↓
continue serving normally
The important word is:
synchronously
During the save operation, Redis blocks normal command processing.
Imagine:
Client 1 → SET A 10
Client 2 → GET B
Client 3 → SET C 20

          ↓

        SAVE

          ↓

Redis is busy generating snapshot
Commands may have to wait.
Therefore:
Do not use SAVE casually on a production Redis instance with a large dataset.
________________________________________
7. BGSAVE
BGSAVE means:
Background Save
Instead of making the main Redis process perform the entire snapshot operation itself, Redis uses a background child process.
Conceptually:
             Redis
              │
           BGSAVE
              │
            fork()
              │
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       │             │
   Parent          Child
   Redis           snapshot
       │             │
       │             ↓
       │          dump.rdb
       │
   continues
   serving clients
This is the important architecture.
________________________________________
8. Why fork()?
On Unix-like operating systems, Redis can use:
fork()
to create a child process.
Initially:
Parent Redis
and:
Child Redis
appear to have the same memory contents.
But Redis does not simply copy the entire dataset immediately.
That would be expensive.
Instead, the operating system uses:
Copy-on-Write
This is one of the most important concepts in Redis internals.
________________________________________
9. Copy-on-Write
Suppose Redis has:
8 GB dataset
If fork() literally copied everything:
Parent → 8 GB
Child  → 8 GB
you'd need:
16 GB
immediately.
That would be terrible.
Instead, initially:
Parent
   │
   ├────────┐
   │        │
   ↓        ↓
same physical memory pages
Both processes initially reference the same memory pages.
Conceptually:
Parent ───────┐
              ├──→ Page A
Child  ───────┘

Parent ───────┐
              ├──→ Page B
Child  ───────┘

Parent ───────┐
              ├──→ Page C
Child  ───────┘
No immediate full copy.
________________________________________
10. What happens when Redis writes after fork?
Now imagine:
BGSAVE
 ↓
fork()
 ↓
child is generating snapshot
Meanwhile the application sends:
SET user:101 "NewValue"
The parent Redis process needs to modify a memory page.
Because the child may still need the old version for its snapshot, the OS performs:
Copy-on-Write
Conceptually:
Before write:

Parent ──┐
          ├──→ Page A
Child  ──┘


Parent modifies Page A

          Page A
             ↑
           Child

New Page A'
             ↑
           Parent
Now:
Parent → modified page
Child  → original page
________________________________________
11. Why is Copy-on-Write useful?
Because Redis can do:
fork()
without immediately copying the entire dataset.
So:
8 GB Redis dataset
doesn't instantly become:
16 GB
just because BGSAVE started.
But...
This does NOT mean BGSAVE is free.
And this is where many production Redis memory problems come from.
________________________________________
12. The Copy-on-Write Memory Trap
Suppose:
Redis dataset = 10 GB
BGSAVE starts:
Parent
Child
Initially:
shared pages
Now the application performs a huge number of writes.
Those writes modify many memory pages.
Copy-on-Write starts creating private copies.
Conceptually:
Initially:

Parent ─┐
        ├── shared pages
Child  ─┘


After many writes:

Parent ──→ modified copies
Child  ───→ original pages
Memory usage can increase significantly.
Potentially:
Original dataset = 10 GB

Additional CoW pages = several GB

Total physical memory pressure ≈ much higher
The exact increase depends on how much memory is modified while the snapshot is running, not simply the dataset size.
________________________________________
13. Extremely Important Interview Statement
Don't say:
“BGSAVE doubles Redis memory.”
That's incorrect.
Instead say:
BGSAVE uses fork and Copy-on-Write. Initially parent and child share memory pages, but writes performed by the parent can cause pages to be copied, increasing physical memory usage. The increase depends on the write workload during the snapshot.
That's the strong interview answer.
________________________________________
14. Parent vs Child Responsibilities
During BGSAVE:
                  Redis
                    │
                  fork()
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          │                   │
        Parent              Child
          │                   │
          │                   │
    serves clients       creates RDB
          │                   │
    SET / GET / DEL             ↓
          │                 dump.rdb
          │
          └───────────────┐
                          │
                    child exits
The parent is the actual Redis server handling client requests.
The child is primarily responsible for generating the snapshot.
________________________________________
15. What exactly does the child write?
The child walks through the Redis dataset and serializes the data into the RDB format.
Conceptually:
Redis memory
    ↓
child process
    ↓
serialize dataset
    ↓
RDB format
    ↓
temporary RDB file
    ↓
final RDB file
The resulting file is commonly named:
dump.rdb
depending on configuration.
________________________________________
16. Is RDB just a text dump?
No.
This is important.
RDB is a binary serialization format.
It is designed to be compact and efficient for persistence and loading.
Conceptually:
Redis objects
     ↓
RDB serialization
     ↓
binary dump
It's not like:
user:101=Gopal
user:102=John
in a simple text file.
________________________________________
17. Why RDB can be fast to restore
Suppose Redis crashes.
With RDB:
Redis starts
   ↓
read RDB file
   ↓
reconstruct in-memory dataset
   ↓
ready
This can be efficient because the RDB file represents a serialized snapshot rather than millions/billions of individual commands that must be replayed.
________________________________________
18. But RDB has a durability tradeoff
Suppose:
RDB snapshot
10:00
Then Redis receives:
10:01 → SET A
10:02 → SET B
10:03 → SET C
10:04 → crash
If the last RDB snapshot was at:
10:00
then after restart:
A
B
C
may be missing.
Because they were not included in the last snapshot.
So:
RDB
 ↓
excellent snapshot
 ↓
but potentially loses changes since last snapshot
________________________________________
19. RDB Persistence Window
Imagine:
10:00 ─────────────── 10:10
 │                       │
RDB snapshot           crash
Changes from:
10:00 → 10:10
may be lost.
Therefore the possible data-loss window is related to how frequently snapshots are taken and whether a newer snapshot completed successfully.
This is called:
RPO — Recovery Point Objective
For example:
RPO = up to 5 minutes
means you're accepting the possibility of losing around five minutes of writes in a failure scenario.
________________________________________
20. Redis Automatic RDB Snapshots
Redis can be configured to automatically perform snapshots based on changes.
A configuration can conceptually say:
save 900 1
save 300 10
save 60 10000
Meaning approximately:
if at least 1 change occurs within 900 seconds
    → snapshot


if at least 10 changes occur within 300 seconds
    → snapshot


if at least 10000 changes occur within 60 seconds
    → snapshot
The exact configuration and semantics should be checked against the Redis version/configuration you're running, but the important concept is:
RDB can be triggered based on elapsed time + number of changes.
________________________________________
21. Why Multiple SAVE Rules?
Because workloads differ.
Suppose Redis receives very few writes:
1 write / 15 minutes
You may still want periodic snapshots.
But with heavy writes:
100,000 writes / minute
you may want more frequent snapshots.
So Redis can express multiple conditions.
Conceptually:
              Changes
                 ↑
                 │
                 │      aggressive writes
                 │          /
                 │        /
                 │      /
                 │    /
                 └────────────→ Time
________________________________________
22. What happens when BGSAVE is triggered automatically?
Conceptually:
Redis event loop
      ↓
snapshot condition met
      ↓
BGSAVE
      ↓
fork()
      ↓
child creates snapshot
      ↓
parent continues serving clients
So automatic RDB persistence generally uses the background save mechanism.
________________________________________
23. BGSAVE and Event Loop
Remember our Lesson 01.
Redis uses an event-driven architecture.
The main Redis process needs to continue processing:
GET
SET
DEL
EXPIRE
etc.
Therefore:
Main process
     ↓
event loop
     ↓
client commands
must remain responsive.
That's why BGSAVE moves the expensive snapshot generation into a child process.
________________________________________
24. But fork() itself isn't free
This is another important production detail.
People sometimes say:
“BGSAVE doesn't block Redis because it happens in a child.”
That's too simplistic.
fork() itself can cause latency impact because the OS has to create process state and establish the Copy-on-Write page mappings.
With very large Redis processes, this can contribute to latency spikes.
So:
BGSAVE
 ≠
zero impact
Instead:
BGSAVE
 ↓
mostly asynchronous snapshot generation
+
fork overhead
+
Copy-on-Write memory pressure
+
disk I/O
________________________________________
25. Disk I/O
The child needs to write:
dump.rdb
to disk.
Suppose:
Redis dataset = 20 GB
Creating a snapshot means significant disk activity.
So you have:
RAM
 ↓
serialization
 ↓
disk writes
This can compete with other system resources.
Especially:
CPU
Memory
Disk bandwidth
Disk latency
________________________________________
26. RDB and Large Datasets
Suppose:
Redis memory = 100 GB
BGSAVE starts.
Potential issues include:
1. fork latency
2. Copy-on-Write memory growth
3. CPU consumed by serialization
4. disk bandwidth consumption
5. longer snapshot duration
6. more pages modified while snapshot runs
And there is a feedback loop:
larger dataset
     ↓
longer snapshot
     ↓
more time for writes
     ↓
more CoW pages
     ↓
more memory pressure
This is a very useful system-design insight.
________________________________________
27. RDB Snapshot Duration Matters
Imagine:
Dataset = 1 GB
snapshot = 2 seconds
versus:
Dataset = 100 GB
snapshot = several minutes
During the second scenario:
more writes happen
       ↓
more pages modified
       ↓
more Copy-on-Write
       ↓
higher memory overhead
So snapshot duration is not merely a disk-performance concern.
It can affect memory usage too.
________________________________________
28. What happens if Redis crashes during BGSAVE?
The parent and child are separate processes.
If the child fails:
Child
 ↓
snapshot failure
the existing Redis process can generally continue serving clients.
The previous valid RDB snapshot remains available if the new snapshot wasn't successfully completed.
This is one reason snapshot generation uses a temporary/atomic replacement approach rather than blindly destroying the previous valid snapshot first.
________________________________________
29. Why Temporary RDB Files?
Imagine:
Existing valid dump.rdb
Redis is creating a new snapshot.
If Redis directly overwrote the only copy and crashed halfway:
dump.rdb
   ↓
half-written
   ↓
corrupted
That would be dangerous.
Instead the conceptual flow is:
Existing dump.rdb
        │
        │
create new temporary RDB
        ↓
dump.rdb.tmp
        ↓
successfully complete
        ↓
rename/replace
        ↓
new dump.rdb
So the old valid snapshot can remain available until the new one is ready.
________________________________________
30. RDB Startup Recovery
Suppose Redis crashes.
At restart:
Redis starts
    ↓
look for RDB
    ↓
load snapshot
    ↓
reconstruct objects
    ↓
database becomes available
Example:
dump.rdb

user:101 → Gopal
user:102 → John
cart:101 → ...
Redis reconstructs:
RAM
│
├── user:101
├── user:102
└── cart:101
________________________________________
31. What About Expiration Information?
This connects directly to Lesson 04.
Suppose:
session:123
value = abc
TTL = 60 sec
When Redis creates the RDB snapshot, expiration metadata is persisted as part of the snapshot information.
After restart, Redis can determine whether the key has already expired based on the stored expiration timestamp.
Conceptually:
RDB
 ├── key
 ├── value
 └── expiration metadata
This is important because persistence doesn't just store values blindly; expiration state matters too.
________________________________________
32. Time and Restart
Suppose:
Snapshot created:
10:00

Key expiration:
10:05
Redis crashes at:
10:03
and restarts at:
10:06
The key's expiration timestamp is already in the past.
Therefore Redis should treat it as expired rather than resurrecting it indefinitely.
This is an important interaction between:
RDB
+
expiration
________________________________________
33. RDB Advantages
RDB is attractive because:
1. Compact snapshots
A snapshot represents the dataset efficiently.
2. Fast restart
Load one snapshot rather than replaying a potentially huge command history.
3. Good backup mechanism
You can copy/store RDB snapshots.
4. Low ongoing overhead
Unlike logging every write, RDB doesn't need to append every command to a persistence log.
5. Point-in-time backups
Snapshots are convenient for backup/restore workflows.
________________________________________
34. RDB Disadvantages
1. Data loss window
Writes since the last successful snapshot can be lost.
2. fork overhead
Large datasets can make fork() expensive.
3. Copy-on-Write memory overhead
Heavy writes during BGSAVE can increase memory usage.
4. Disk I/O
Snapshot generation can consume substantial disk bandwidth.
5. Snapshot duration
Large datasets take longer to serialize.
________________________________________
35. RDB vs AOF — First Look
We will study AOF deeply later, but understand the fundamental distinction now.
RDB
 ↓
snapshot
 ↓
"What does Redis look like at this point?"


AOF
 ↓
operation log
 ↓
"What operations happened?"
Example:
SET A 10
SET B 20
INCR A
DEL B
RDB eventually stores the resulting state:
A → 11
AOF conceptually records commands that can reconstruct that state.
________________________________________
36. Durability Trade-off
Think of this triangle:
             Durability
                /\
               /  \
              /    \
             /      \
            /        \
      Performance ---- Recovery
You can't optimize every dimension perfectly.
RDB chooses a strategy roughly like:
efficient snapshots
+
fast recovery
-
possible write-loss window
AOF provides a different tradeoff.
________________________________________
37. System Design Scenario
Imagine you're designing:
E-commerce application
        ↓
Redis
        ↓
PostgreSQL
Redis stores:
product cache
session data
recommendation cache
Do you necessarily need RDB?
Not always.
If Redis is purely a cache:
Redis data
    ↓
rebuildable from DB
then persistence may be less important.
If Redis stores:
shopping cart
distributed state
important counters
queues
then losing data can be much more serious.
So persistence strategy depends on:
What does Redis represent in your architecture?
________________________________________
38. Cache vs Primary Data Store
This is an important system-design distinction.
Redis as cache
PostgreSQL
    ↓
source of truth

Redis
    ↓
performance layer
If Redis loses everything:
repopulate from PostgreSQL
The impact is mostly:
cache miss storm
+
database load
Redis as primary/important state
Redis
 ↓
important application state
Then losing recent writes could mean:
actual business data loss
Persistence becomes much more important.
________________________________________
39. RDB and Cache Stampede Connection
Remember our previous lesson.
Suppose Redis restarts and loads an old RDB.
Some cache entries may be missing or stale.
Then:
Redis restart
    ↓
many cache misses
    ↓
database load
    ↓
potential stampede
So persistence, expiration, and cache architecture interact.
They aren't isolated topics.
