41. Most Important Interview Questions
Q1. What is RDB?
RDB is Redis's point-in-time snapshot persistence mechanism. It periodically serializes the in-memory dataset into an RDB file.
Q2. SAVE vs BGSAVE?
SAVE
 ↓
synchronous
 ↓
blocks Redis during snapshot


BGSAVE
 ↓
background child process
 ↓
parent continues serving clients
Q3. Why does Redis use fork for BGSAVE?
To create a child process that can generate the snapshot while the parent continues serving clients.
Q4. Does fork immediately duplicate all Redis memory?
No. The OS uses Copy-on-Write, so parent and child initially share memory pages.
Q5. When does memory increase?
When the parent modifies shared pages during the snapshot, those pages may need to be copied, increasing physical memory usage.
Q6. Does BGSAVE have zero performance impact?
No. Fork overhead, serialization CPU, disk I/O, and Copy-on-Write memory pressure can affect the system.
Q7. What happens to writes after the snapshot starts?
The parent continues accepting writes. Copy-on-Write ensures the child can see the appropriate snapshot state while the parent continues modifying its own pages.
Q8. What is the major RDB weakness?
Potential loss of writes since the last successful snapshot.
________________________________________
42. One Critical Correction for Your Mental Model
Don't think:
BGSAVE
 ↓
copy all Redis memory
 ↓
write copy to disk
Think:
BGSAVE
 ↓
fork()
 ↓
parent + child initially share pages
 ↓
child serializes snapshot
 ↓
parent continues writes
 ↓
modified pages trigger Copy-on-Write
 ↓
child finishes RDB
This is the model you should carry into system-design interviews.
