1. First: What problem is AOF solving?
Remember the fundamental Redis architecture:
Application
     |
     v
   Redis
     |
     v
   RAM
Redis primarily stores data in memory.
That gives us:
RAM → extremely fast
But RAM is volatile.
If Redis crashes:
Redis process
     X
     |
     v
   RAM lost
So we need persistence.
There are two major strategies:
RDB
 └── periodically save a snapshot

AOF
 └── record the operations that changed the data
The mental model is:
RDB
"What did Redis's state look like at a particular point in time?"
AOF
"What operations happened to Redis?"
This distinction is extremely important.
________________________________________
2. The fundamental idea behind AOF
Suppose we execute:
SET user:101 Gopal
SET user:101 Krishna
INCR counter
DEL user:101
Redis changes its in-memory state.
AOF says:
Don't just remember the final state. Record the commands that produced the state.
Conceptually:
AOF file

SET user:101 Gopal
SET user:101 Krishna
INCR counter
DEL user:101
If Redis crashes, it can:
Start Redis
     |
     v
Read AOF
     |
     v
Replay commands
     |
     v
Reconstruct RAM
So:
AOF = command log
This is similar to a concept you'll see repeatedly in distributed systems:
Event/log
   ↓
Replay
   ↓
Reconstruct state
That connection is important for system design.
________________________________________
3. RDB vs AOF — the core difference
Suppose these commands happen:
SET x 10
SET x 20
SET x 30
Final state:
x = 30
RDB
A snapshot might contain:
x = 30
It doesn't care about the history.
AOF
Initially:
SET x 10
SET x 20
SET x 30
It records the history of modifications.
Therefore:
RDB → state
AOF → operations
This is analogous to:
Database snapshot
       vs
Write-ahead/log-based history
________________________________________
4. But wait — does Redis literally store my command?
This is where implementation knowledge matters.
AOF stores commands in a Redis serialization format based on the RESP protocol.
For example:
SET user:101 Gopal
is represented roughly as:
*3
$3
SET
$8
user:101
$6
Gopal
So don't imagine the AOF as simply:
SET user:101 Gopal
It is encoded so Redis can efficiently parse and replay it.
________________________________________
5. The most important AOF question
Suppose Redis receives:
SET x 100
What happens?
Think about the pipeline:
Client
  |
  v
Redis
  |
  +----> Execute command
  |          |
  |          v
  |        RAM
  |
  +----> Append command to AOF buffer
There is an important intermediate concept:
AOF Buffer
Redis doesn't necessarily perform a disk write for every command immediately.
Instead:
Command
   |
   v
Execute
   |
   +----> RAM
   |
   +----> AOF buffer
              |
              v
           fsync/write
              |
              v
             Disk
This distinction is critical.
________________________________________
6. write() vs fsync()
This is where many developers misunderstand Redis persistence.
There are multiple layers:
Redis process
      |
      v
OS page cache
      |
      v
Disk
When Redis writes to a file, the data may initially exist in the operating system's page cache rather than physically on the disk.
Conceptually:
Redis
  |
  | write()
  v
OS page cache
  |
  | fsync()
  v
Disk
So:
write()
means approximately:
"OS, please accept these bytes."
fsync()
means approximately:
"OS, please flush this file's buffered data to stable storage."
This creates the central AOF trade-off:
Durability
    vs
Performance
________________________________________
7. The three AOF fsync policies
Redis provides:
appendfsync always
appendfsync everysec
appendfsync no
These are extremely important for interviews.
________________________________________
8. appendfsync always
Conceptually:
Command
   |
   v
Execute
   |
   v
Append AOF
   |
   v
fsync
   |
   v
Return
Every write is synchronized to disk.
Advantage
Very strong durability.
Disadvantage
Very expensive.
Imagine:
100,000 writes/sec
If every operation forces disk synchronization:
100,000 fsync operations/sec
That can dramatically reduce throughput and increase latency.
So:
Durability ↑
Performance ↓
You generally don't choose this unless your durability requirements justify the cost.
________________________________________
9. appendfsync everysec
This is the commonly used compromise.
Conceptually:
Commands
   |
   v
AOF buffer
   |
   v
write
   |
   v
OS cache
   |
   |
 every ~1 sec
   |
   v
 fsync
   |
   v
 Disk
Redis can continue processing commands while the OS/disk synchronization happens approximately once per second.
Therefore:
Performance = good
Durability = good
But there's a critical consequence.
If Redis crashes before the latest buffered data reaches stable storage:
Potentially ~1 second of writes lost
Not necessarily exactly one second—it's better to think:
With everysec, Redis generally targets fsync about once per second, so a crash can lose recent writes that had not yet been fsynced.
________________________________________
10. appendfsync no
Here Redis delegates synchronization to the operating system.
Conceptually:
Redis
  |
  v
OS
  |
  v
whenever OS decides
  |
  v
Disk
Redis doesn't explicitly force fsync at the configured interval.
Performance
Potentially better.
Durability
Less predictable.
If the machine crashes:
recent writes
     ↓
may still be in OS cache
     ↓
lost
So the three policies can be remembered as:
always
  ↓
maximum durability
minimum performance

everysec
  ↓
balanced

no
  ↓
maximum OS-dependent buffering
weakest durability guarantee
________________________________________
11. A very important distinction
Don't say in an interview:
"AOF everysec guarantees no more than one second of data loss."
That's too absolute.
A better answer:
"everysec asks Redis to fsync approximately once per second, so under normal conditions the durability window is roughly one second, but actual loss can depend on OS, filesystem, storage, and failure mode."
That's the kind of answer expected from someone with production experience.
________________________________________
12. Now let's discuss AOF growth
Suppose we execute:
SET x 10
SET x 20
SET x 30
SET x 40
SET x 50
AOF could contain:
SET x 10
SET x 20
SET x 30
SET x 40
SET x 50
But Redis only needs:
x = 50
The previous operations are no longer necessary to reconstruct the current state.
This creates a problem:
AOF can grow indefinitely.
For example:
Day 1 → 1 GB
Day 2 → 3 GB
Day 3 → 7 GB
Day 4 → 15 GB
Even if actual Redis data is only:
1 GB
the AOF could become much larger.
So Redis needs:
AOF Rewrite
________________________________________
13. AOF Rewrite
AOF rewrite means:
Create a new, smaller AOF that represents the current Redis state.
Suppose old AOF contains:
SET x 10
SET x 20
SET x 30
SET x 40
SET x 50
After rewrite, Redis can produce something equivalent to:
SET x 50
Therefore:
Old AOF
10 GB
   |
   | rewrite
   v
New AOF
2 GB
The important insight:
AOF rewrite is not rewriting the history. It is generating the minimal command sequence needed to reconstruct the current state.
________________________________________
14. But how can Redis rewrite while serving traffic?
This is the really interesting part.
Imagine Redis has:
100 GB
and clients continue writing while rewrite is happening.
If Redis simply stopped accepting writes:
clients
   |
   X

rewrite
that would be terrible.
Redis therefore performs the rewrite in the background.
Historically this uses a child process:
              Redis parent
                  |
          -----------------
          |               |
       client          child process
       traffic         AOF rewrite
          |               |
          |               v
          |          temporary file
          |
          v
        RAM
The parent continues serving clients.
________________________________________
15. The tricky part: writes during rewrite
Suppose rewrite starts when:
x = 10
Child process begins generating:
SET x 10
But while rewrite is happening, clients execute:
SET x 20
SET y 100
DEL z
The child process started from an earlier state.
So how do we avoid losing these new commands?
Redis maintains additional information about writes occurring during the rewrite.
Conceptually:
                Redis parent
                    |
                    +------> normal AOF
                    |
                    +------> rewrite buffer
                                  |
                                  v
                             new commands
The child generates the base AOF.
Meanwhile:
client writes
     |
     v
parent
     |
     +----> normal processing
     |
     +----> accumulate changes
After the child finishes, Redis incorporates those changes into the new AOF.
This allows the new AOF to represent:
state at rewrite start
+
writes during rewrite
________________________________________
16. Why fork matters
On Unix-like systems, Redis can use:
fork()
The parent and child initially share memory pages using:
Copy-on-Write
This is a major system-design concept.
Suppose Redis has:
RAM
------------------
user:1
user:2
user:3
...
millions of keys
Redis forks.
Initially:
Parent --------\
                > shared memory pages
Child --------/
The OS doesn't immediately copy everything.
That's why fork is relatively efficient compared with immediately duplicating the entire dataset.
But if the parent modifies a memory page:
Parent writes
     |
     v
page must be copied
This is:
Copy-on-Write
Therefore background persistence can still create memory pressure.
________________________________________
17. This is a production-important issue
Imagine:
Redis dataset = 50 GB
A rewrite starts.
Parent continues modifying data.
Because of Copy-on-Write, memory usage can increase significantly depending on write activity.
So you may see:
Redis memory
     |
     +---- dataset
     |
     +---- COW overhead
     |
     +---- child process needs
This is one reason why:
"Redis uses RAM, therefore just give it 50 GB RAM"
is not sufficient capacity planning.
You need headroom.
________________________________________
18. AOF rewrite vs RDB snapshot
Now compare the mechanisms.
RDB
Redis state
    |
    v
fork
    |
    v
child
    |
    v
snapshot file
AOF rewrite
Redis state
    |
    v
fork
    |
    v
child
    |
    v
generate compact command representation
Both can involve:
fork
Copy-on-Write
background work
additional memory pressure
But their output is different:
RDB → binary snapshot

AOF → command-based log representation
