19. AOF rewrite does NOT mean "read old AOF and compress it"
This is a common misconception.
Suppose old AOF contains:
SET x 1
SET x 2
SET x 3
SET x 4
Rewrite doesn't necessarily do:
read every old command
remove redundant commands
write new AOF
Instead, the rewrite can inspect the current Redis data structures and generate commands representing the current state.
So conceptually:
Current RAM
    |
    v
iterate dataset
    |
    v
generate compact commands
    |
    v
new AOF
This is much more important than simply thinking "AOF compression."
________________________________________
20. AOF rewrite and durability
Another subtle point.
During rewrite:
Old AOF
     |
     | still important
     v
Current durability history
while:
New AOF
     |
     | being generated
     v
temporary file
You cannot simply destroy the old AOF before the new representation is safely ready.
Otherwise:
old AOF deleted
new AOF incomplete
     |
     v
crash
     |
     v
data recovery problem
So Redis has to carefully coordinate:
old AOF
new temporary AOF
writes during rewrite
atomic transition
This is a general distributed/storage-system principle:
Never replace a known-good persistent representation with an incomplete one.
________________________________________
21. AOF corruption after crash
Consider:
Redis
  |
  v
AOF append
  |
  v
CRASH
The last AOF command may have been partially written.
For example, conceptually:
*3
$3
SET
$5
user1
$...
and the process crashes halfway through.
Now the AOF may have an incomplete final command.
Redis provides mechanisms/options to deal with AOF corruption and recovery.
The important conceptual lesson is:
Persistence systems must handle partially written records.
This problem exists far beyond Redis:
Redis AOF
database WAL
Kafka logs
filesystem journals
________________________________________
22. AOF is similar to a WAL — but don't say they're identical
A useful mental model:
AOF ≈ command log
WAL  ≈ write-ahead log
They share ideas:
record changes
persist log
