07 — Redis Replication
Since you're learning this for system design, don't think of replication as:
"Redis copies data from one server to another."
That's only the surface.
The deeper question is:
How does Redis keep another machine sufficiently up-to-date when writes are continuously happening, connections fail, and replicas fall behind?
That leads us to replication offsets, replication backlog, PSYNC, full sync, partial sync, and asynchronous consistency.
________________________________________
1. Why do we need replication?
Start with a single Redis server:
             Application
                  |
                  v
            +-----------+
            |   Redis   |
            |  Primary  |
            +-----------+
                  |
                 Disk
What happens if the machine dies?
             Application
                  |
                  X
                  |
            Redis DEAD
Persistence helps if we restart Redis, but recovery takes time.
We want another Redis server already holding the data:
                    Application
                         |
                         v
                  +-------------+
                  |   Primary   |
                  +-------------+
                    |
                    | replicate
                    v
                  +-------------+
                  |   Replica   |
                  +-------------+
Now if the primary fails:
Primary
   X
   |
   v
Replica
   |
   v
continue serving
So the first purpose of replication is:
High availability / redundancy
But that's only one reason.
Replication can also help with:
•	read scaling 
•	geographic distribution 
•	reducing load on primary 
•	failover architecture 
•	disaster recovery 
________________________________________
2. Primary and Replica
Redis uses the terms:
Primary
Replica
Older Redis documentation commonly used:
Master
Slave
You may still see those terms in old documentation/configuration.
Our architecture:
                 Clients
                    |
                    v
              +-----------+
              |  Primary  |
              +-----------+
               /         \
              /           \
             v             v
      +-----------+   +-----------+
      | Replica 1 |   | Replica 2 |
      +-----------+   +-----------+
The primary accepts writes.
Replicas normally receive those writes through replication.
________________________________________
3. The fundamental replication flow
Suppose the application executes:
SET user:101 Gopal
The flow is conceptually:
Client
  |
  v
Primary
  |
  +----> execute command
  |
  +----> replicate write
             |
             +----> Replica 1
             |
             +----> Replica 2
Notice something important:
The primary doesn't send:
"Here is the entire Redis database again."
for every command.
It sends the changes through the replication stream.
________________________________________
4. Is Redis replication synchronous?
This is one of the most important interview questions.
Redis replication is primarily:
Asynchronous
Suppose:
Client
   |
   v
Primary
   |
   | SET x 100
   |
   +------> Replica
The primary can acknowledge the client without waiting for every replica to persist/process the command.
Conceptually:
Client
  |
  | SET x 100
  v
Primary
  |
  | ACK client
  |
  +------------> Replica
Therefore:
Primary accepted write
        ≠
Replica definitely has write
That difference creates a replication lag window.
________________________________________
5. Replication lag
Imagine:
Time →

Primary:
SET x 1
SET x 2
SET x 3
SET x 4
SET x 5

Replica:
SET x 1
SET x 2
SET x 3
The replica is behind.
We call this:
Replication lag
Conceptually:
Primary position = 1000
Replica position = 970

lag = 30 units
We'll soon see that Redis uses replication offsets to reason about this.
________________________________________
6. Why asynchronous replication?
You might ask:
Why not wait for the replica before acknowledging every write?
Because that would add network latency.
Imagine:
Application
    |
    | 1 ms
    v
Primary
    |
    | 50 ms
    v
Replica
If every write requires replica confirmation:
write latency
≈ primary processing
 + network
 + replica processing
 + acknowledgement
Now Redis becomes constrained by replica/network latency.
With asynchronous replication:
Application
    |
    | 1 ms
    v
Primary
    |
    +---- ACK immediately
    |
    +---- replicate asynchronously
This gives:
Lower write latency
        +
Higher throughput
at the cost of:
Potential replication lag
Potential recent-write loss during failover
That is a classic distributed-systems trade-off.
________________________________________
7. The big question: how does a replica catch up?
Suppose a replica has just started.
It has:
nothing
while the primary has:
1 billion keys
You cannot just send:
SET key1...
SET key2...
...
indefinitely while the system is changing.
Redis therefore has synchronization mechanisms.
There are two major cases:
Replica synchronization
       |
       +----------------+
       |                |
       v                v
   Full Sync        Partial Sync
Let's understand them deeply.
________________________________________
8. Full synchronization
Suppose a new replica joins:
Primary
   |
   | "I have no data"
   |
   v
Replica
The primary may perform a:
Full synchronization
Conceptually:
Primary
   |
   | create snapshot
   v
RDB representation
   |
   | send
   v
Replica
   |
   v
load dataset
But here's the interesting part.
What happens to writes occurring while the snapshot is being created?
For example:
Initial state:
x = 10
Full sync starts.
While synchronization is happening:
SET x 20
SET y 30
SET z 40
Those writes cannot simply disappear.
Redis therefore maintains replication information for commands that occur while the replica is catching up.
Conceptually:
                 Primary
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
          v                   v
       snapshot          new writes
          |                   |
          |                   |
          v                   v
       Replica <------- catch-up
This is one reason the replication stream/backlog is important.
________________________________________
9. Full sync step-by-step
Let's simplify the process.
Step 1 — Replica connects
Replica
   |
   | connect
   v
Primary
Step 2 — Replica tells primary:
Conceptually:
"I need synchronization."
Step 3 — Primary determines:
Can I provide only the missing commands?
If not:
Full synchronization required
Step 4 — Primary generates a snapshot
Conceptually:
Current Redis state
       |
       v
RDB snapshot
Step 5 — Snapshot is sent
Primary
   |
   | RDB
   v
Replica
Step 6 — Replica loads it
RDB
 ↓
RAM
Step 7 — Replica catches up with writes that happened after the snapshot point
Snapshot
   +
subsequent replication stream
   ↓
Current replica state
Now:
Primary ≈ Replica
________________________________________
10. Why full synchronization is expensive
Imagine:
Redis dataset = 100 GB
A new replica requires:
100 GB transfer
plus:
disk I/O
CPU
network bandwidth
replica loading
During that period, the primary is still serving production traffic.
So frequent full synchronization is undesirable.
This leads to a much more interesting mechanism:
Partial synchronization
________________________________________
11. Partial synchronization
Imagine:
Primary:
A B C D E F G H I J
Replica currently has:
A B C D E F
The network connection temporarily fails.
While disconnected, primary receives:
G
H
I
The replica reconnects.
Do we need to send the entire dataset?
No.
If the primary still knows the missing changes:
G H I
it can send only those.
So:
Partial synchronization
means roughly:
"Send the replica only the portion of the replication stream it missed."
This is dramatically cheaper than full synchronization.
________________________________________
12. Replication backlog
Now we reach one of the most important Redis concepts.
How can the primary remember changes that a disconnected replica missed?
Redis maintains a:
Replication backlog
Think of it as a circular buffer containing recent replication data.
Conceptually:
Primary
   |
   v
+--------------------------------+
| replication backlog            |
|                                |
| old commands ---> new commands |
+--------------------------------+
Suppose:
SET A 1
SET B 2
SET C 3
SET D 4
SET E 5
The backlog might contain recent commands.
If a replica disconnects briefly:
Primary continues
       |
       v
backlog stores recent changes
When replica reconnects:
Replica:
"I missed these changes."

Primary:
"I still have them in backlog."

        ↓

Send missing portion
That's partial synchronization.
________________________________________
13. Why is the backlog a circular buffer?
Because we don't want it to grow forever.
Imagine Redis writes:
millions of commands/sec
If backlog stored every historical command forever:
memory
  ↑
  |
  |
  +-------------------->
          time
Eventually impossible.
Instead:
+-----------------------+
| recent history        |
+-----------------------+
When it fills:
oldest data overwritten
That's why it's called a:
Circular buffer
Conceptually:
        +---------+
        | command |
        v         |
   +----+----+----+
   | A | B | C | D |
   +---+---+---+---+
       ^
       |
     newest
New entries overwrite old entries.
Therefore:
The replication backlog can only help a replica that has fallen behind within the backlog's retained history.
________________________________________
14. This creates a critical decision
Suppose:
Replication backlog = 1 MB
Primary produces:
10 MB/sec
Replica disconnects for:
5 seconds
During that time:
10 MB/sec × 5 sec
= 50 MB
Replica missed approximately:
50 MB
But backlog only retains:
1 MB
Therefore:
Required missing data = 50 MB
Available backlog = 1 MB

→ partial resync impossible
→ full synchronization required
This is why backlog sizing matters in production.
________________________________________
15. Replication offset
Now we need a way to identify where a replica is in the stream.
Redis uses a:
Replication offset
Imagine the primary's replication stream as a sequence of bytes:
0
|
v
+---------------------------------------------------->
| command bytes                                      |
+---------------------------------------------------->
                                                  1,000,000
Suppose:
Primary offset = 1,000,000
Replica offset = 999,000
Then approximately:
Replica is 1,000 bytes behind
Conceptually:
Primary
offset = 1,000,000

Replica
offset =   999,000

difference = 1,000
This is much more useful than saying:
"Replica is 17 commands behind."
Why?
Because commands have different sizes.
For example:
SET x 1
and:
SET extremely-long-key some-extremely-long-value...
have very different byte lengths.
Offsets track the replication stream position.
________________________________________
16. Think of replication as a log
This is the mental model I want you to keep:
Primary produces a stream:

0 ---------------------------------------------------->
       replication bytes

Replica consumes the stream:

0 ----------------------------->
                         ^
                    replica offset
So:
Primary = producer
Replica = consumer
Backlog = recent retained stream
Offset = position in stream
This is conceptually very similar to ideas you'll later see in:
Kafka
log replication
WAL
distributed consensus
event streams
That's why learning Redis replication deeply helps with broader system design.
________________________________________
17. PSYNC
Now Redis needs a protocol to ask:
"Can I continue from where I left off?"
This is where:
PSYNC
comes in.
Historically, Redis introduced PSYNC to support partial synchronization.
Conceptually:
Replica
   |
   | PSYNC
   | "My replication state is here"
   v
Primary
Primary checks whether it can satisfy that request from its retained replication history.
Two broad outcomes:
PSYNC
  |
  +----> Partial resynchronization possible
  |
  +----> Full synchronization required
________________________________________
18. Replication ID
Offsets alone aren't enough.
Imagine:
Primary A
offset = 5000
Then primary is restarted/replaced and another replication history begins:
Primary B
offset = 5000
The number 5000 alone doesn't tell us whether they're the same history.
Therefore Redis uses a:
Replication ID
Conceptually:
Replication history A
ID = abc123...
offset = 5000
Replica remembers:
I have replication history abc123...
up to offset 5000
When reconnecting:
Replica
   |
   | "I have ID abc123, offset 5000"
   v
Primary
Primary can determine whether the requested history is still available.
________________________________________
19. So partial synchronization requires matching history
Think:
Replica says:

Replication ID = X
Offset = 5000
Primary asks:
Do I still have replication history X
and bytes after offset 5000?
If yes:
→ partial synchronization
If no:
→ full synchronization
This is a beautiful distributed-systems pattern:
identity of history
+
position in history
+
retained history
________________________________________
20. The complete synchronization decision
Now put everything together:
Replica reconnects
        |
        v
       PSYNC
        |
        v
Does primary recognize
replication history?
        |
     +--+--+
     |     |
    YES    NO
     |     |
     v     v
Is missing    FULL SYNC
data still
in backlog?
     |
   +--+--+
   |     |
  YES    NO
   |     |
   v     v
PARTIAL  FULL
SYNC     SYNC
This diagram is worth remembering.
