Redis Cluster + Hash Slots — Lesson 1
1. Start with the problem Redis Cluster solves
Imagine your application currently has:
                    Java Application
                          |
                    Redis Primary
                    64 GB RAM
                    100K ops/sec
Eventually you hit one of these limits:
Problem A — Memory
Your Redis dataset becomes:
150 GB
One Redis instance cannot comfortably hold it.
You could add replicas:
             Primary
            /       \
       Replica 1  Replica 2
But replicas don't increase write capacity or total dataset capacity.
All three still contain the same 150 GB.
________________________________________
Problem B — Write throughput
Suppose one Redis primary can handle:
200K writes/sec
but your system needs:
600K writes/sec
Adding replicas doesn't solve this because writes still go to the primary.
You need:
             Redis Cluster

        Primary A
        Primary B
        Primary C

        200K      200K      200K
Now writes can be distributed.
________________________________________
Problem C — Single-node failure
With standalone Redis:
Java
 |
Redis
 X
Your system is unavailable until recovery/failover happens.
With Cluster:
              Redis Cluster

        P1          P2          P3
       /           /           /
     R1           R2          R3
A primary can fail and its replica can potentially become the new primary.
So Redis Cluster gives us roughly:
horizontal partitioning + high availability
The important word is partitioning.
And that's where hash slots enter.
________________________________________
2. Why can't Redis simply use normal hashing?
Suppose we have 3 Redis nodes:
Node A
Node B
Node C
A naïve approach could be:
node = hash(key) % 3;
For example:
user:101 -> hash -> 17 -> 17 % 3 = 2 -> Node C
user:102 -> hash -> 23 -> 23 % 3 = 2 -> Node C
user:103 -> hash -> 41 -> 41 % 3 = 2 -> Node C
Looks simple.
But now imagine adding a fourth node:
hash(key) % 4
Almost everything changes.
Previously:
hash(key) % 3
After:
hash(key) % 4
A huge percentage of keys map to different nodes.
That means massive data movement.
Redis Cluster avoids this using an intermediate abstraction:
Hash Slots
Instead of:
key -> node
Redis Cluster does:
key
 |
hash
 |
slot
 |
node
This is the central idea you need to understand.
________________________________________
3. Redis has 16,384 hash slots
Redis Cluster defines:
16384 slots
Think of them as numbered buckets:
0
1
2
3
...
16382
16383
Every Redis key belongs to exactly one hash slot.
Conceptually:
"user:101"
     |
     v
hash
     |
     v
slot 9182
Another key:
"order:5001"
     |
     v
hash
     |
     v
slot 134
Another:
"product:900"
     |
