11. Redis Cluster — Resharding + Failover
Before going further, keep this mental model:
                    Redis Cluster

       P1                    P2                    P3
   slots 0–5460        slots 5461–10922      slots 10923–16383
       |                     |                     |
      R1                    R2                    R3
•	Primary owns hash slots and handles writes. 
•	Replica copies its primary's data and can take over. 
•	Hash slots are the unit Redis moves during scaling. 
•	Resharding = moving slots between primaries. 
•	Failover = replacing a failed primary with a replica. 
These are different problems.
________________________________________
11.1 Why do we need resharding?
Imagine you started with:
P1 → 0–5460
P2 → 5461–10922
P3 → 10923–16383
Traffic grows.
You add:
P4
You don't want:
P1 → everything
P2 → everything
P3 → everything
P4 → nothing
Instead, you redistribute slots:
Before:

P1 → 0–5460
P2 → 5461–10922
P3 → 10923–16383


After:

P1 → 0–4095
P2 → 4096–8191
P3 → 8192–12287
P4 → 12288–16383
That redistribution is resharding.
The important thing:
Redis moves slots, and therefore the keys belonging to those slots.
________________________________________
11.2 What actually happens during resharding?
Let's say:
P1 owns slot 5000
and we want to move slot 5000 to P4.
Initially:
slot 5000 → P1
Redis begins migration:
P1 ───────────────→ P4
       slot 5000
Conceptually:
P1
 ├── key A
 ├── key B
 ├── key C
 └── key D
Those keys belong to slot 5000.
They are gradually migrated:
P1                 P4

key A ───────────→ key A
key B ───────────→ key B
key C ───────────→ key C
key D ───────────→ key D
Once migration completes:
slot 5000 → P4
________________________________________
11.3 Why can't Redis just pause the application?
Imagine your production system handles:
500,000 requests/sec
You can't say:
"Everyone stop for 30 seconds while I move Redis data."
So resharding is designed to happen while the cluster remains operational.
That is why the MIGRATING / IMPORTING state and ASK redirection exist.
________________________________________
11.4 The ASK scenario
Suppose slot 5000 is migrating:
P1 → P4
Some keys have moved:
P1:
key A
key B

P4:
key C
key D
But the slot isn't completely transferred yet.
Now the application asks for:
GET key C
The client might initially contact P1 because its topology still says:
slot 5000 → P1
P1 knows migration is happening.
It can tell the client:
ASK 5000 P4
Meaning:
"For this request, go ask P4."
The client sends an ASKING command to P4 and retries the operation.
Conceptually:
Java
 |
 | GET key-C
 v
P1
 |
 | ASK 5000 P4
 v
Java
 |
 | ASKING
 | GET key-C
 v
P4
After migration is complete, Redis changes the authoritative slot ownership.
Then clients receive:
MOVED
instead.
________________________________________
11.5 MOVED vs ASK
This distinction is worth memorizing conceptually:
Response	Meaning
MOVED	Slot belongs to another node now
ASK	Slot is currently being migrated
Think:
MOVED = permanent routing change
ASK   = temporary migration routing
A Redis Cluster-aware Java client handles these details.
________________________________________
11.6 What happens when adding a node?
Suppose:
P1
P2
P3
You add:
P4
Adding the process alone isn't enough.
Initially:
P4 → 0 slots
You must assign slots to it.
For example:
P1 → 0–4000
P2 → 4001–8000
P3 → 8001–12000
P4 → 12001–16383
That requires moving data.
So:
Adding node
     ↓
Assign slots
     ↓
Move keys
     ↓
Update cluster topology
This is why horizontal scaling Redis isn't instantaneous.
The new machine can join quickly, but balancing the dataset takes time.
________________________________________
11.7 Now the second half: Failover
Resharding is about:
capacity
Failover is about:
availability
Suppose:
P1
 |
