P2 crashes:
P1       X       P3
 |               |
R1              R3

        R2
What happens to slots owned by P2?
For example:
P2 → slots 5461–10922
Those slots have no primary.
If Redis did nothing:
requests for slots 5461–10922
                 ↓
             unavailable
But Redis Cluster has replicas.
R2 can attempt to become the new primary.
Before:

P2
 |
R2


After failure:

     R2
      ↑
  new primary
Then:
slots 5461–10922 → R2
________________________________________
11.8 But who decides R2 should become primary?
This is where Redis Cluster's distributed coordination comes in.
Redis nodes communicate with each other using the cluster bus.
Conceptually:
P1 ←──────→ P2
 ↕           ↕
P3 ←──────→ R2
They exchange information about:
•	node health 
•	cluster membership 
•	slot ownership 
•	failures 
•	configuration/state 
If P2 stops responding, other nodes can mark it as potentially failed.
There are two useful states to understand:
PFAIL
and
FAIL
________________________________________
11.9 PFAIL
PFAIL means roughly:
"I personally suspect this node has failed."
Suppose P1 cannot communicate with P2.
P1 thinks:
P2 = PFAIL
But P1 doesn't necessarily have enough information to declare the whole cluster that P2 is definitely dead.
Another node might still be able to communicate with P2.
So:
PFAIL
=
local suspicion
________________________________________
11.10 FAIL
Eventually, enough cluster information can establish that the node is actually considered failed by the cluster.
Then:
P2 = FAIL
Think:
PFAIL → local suspicion

FAIL  → cluster-level failure state
That distinction matters because distributed systems cannot safely assume:
"I can't see node X, therefore X is dead."
Maybe the network is broken.
Maybe the node is overloaded.
Maybe there's a partition.
________________________________________
11.11 Replica promotion
Suppose:
P2
 |
R2
P2 fails.
R2 needs to determine whether it can take over.
The goal becomes:
R2 → P2's slots
After successful failover:
P1              R2              P3
 |               |               |
R1              R?              R3

         R2 is now primary
The name doesn't matter conceptually.
Its role changed:
Replica
   ↓
Primary
and it takes ownership of the failed primary's slots.
________________________________________
11.12 Why doesn't every replica immediately become primary?
Because then you could have:
P2 fails

R2 says: "I'm primary!"
R4 says: "I'm primary!"
R5 says: "I'm primary!"
Now multiple nodes believe they own the same slots.
That's dangerous.
You could get:
split brain
where two different nodes accept writes for the same logical partition.
Redis Cluster therefore needs coordination and voting rules around failover.
________________________________________
11.13 Replica election
A simplified mental model:
P2 fails
   |
   v
R2 detects failure
   |
   v
R2 requests votes
   |
   v
Other masters participate
   |
   v
R2 gets sufficient votes
   |
   v
R2 promotes itself
   |
   v
R2 owns P2's slots
This is why Redis Cluster isn't merely:
primary + dumb backup
The cluster has distributed failure detection and promotion logic.
________________________________________
11.14 Why replication lag matters
Now consider this production scenario.
P2 processes:
SET user:123 balance=100
P2's replica R2 hasn't received that update yet.
Then:
P2 crashes
R2 becomes primary.
What value does R2 have?
Potentially:
balance = old value
This exposes an important distributed-systems reality:
Replication does not automatically mean zero data loss.
There can be a window between:
primary accepted write
        ↓
replica received write
If the primary dies during that window, the newest write may be lost.
________________________________________
11.15 This is where system design gets interesting
Suppose your system says:
"Payment balance updates must never be lost."
Would Redis Cluster alone automatically give you that guarantee?
No.
You need to reason about:
•	replication 
•	acknowledgement 
•	failover 
•	durability 
•	application-level transaction boundaries 
•	whether Redis is the source of truth or merely a cache 
For a cache:
lost Redis data
     ↓
rebuild from DB
might be completely acceptable.
For a financial ledger:
lost Redis write
     ↓
potentially catastrophic
So your architecture matters more than the Redis feature itself.
________________________________________
11.16 A very important architecture distinction
Redis as cache
Application
    |
    v
Redis Cluster
    |
    v
Database
If Redis loses data:
Redis failure
    ↓
cache miss
    ↓
Database
    ↓
repopulate Redis
Usually acceptable.
Redis as primary state
Application
    |
    v
Redis Cluster
Now failure semantics become much more important.
You need to ask:
"What consistency and durability guarantees does my application require?"
That's a system-design question, not a Redis syntax question.
________________________________________
11.17 Failover timeline
Let's walk through a realistic example.
Initial state:
P1        P2        P3
|         |         |
R1        R2        R3
P2 owns:
slots 5461–10922
Now:
