12. Redis Transactions + WATCH + Lua
This topic is extremely important because Redis is often used for operations that look like:
read → decide → write
The moment multiple Java requests can touch the same Redis state concurrently, you need to reason about atomicity.
We'll build this from a real problem rather than definitions.
________________________________________
12.1 Start with a race condition
Suppose Redis contains:
balance:1001 = 1000
Two Java requests arrive almost simultaneously.
Request A: withdraw 700
Request B: withdraw 500
Your naïve Java code might do:
int balance = redis.get("balance:1001");

if (balance >= amount) {
    redis.set("balance:1001", balance - amount);
}
Looks reasonable.
But let's put the requests on a timeline.
Request A
GET balance:1001
→ 1000
Request B
GET balance:1001
→ 1000
Both Java threads now believe:
balance = 1000
Then:
A → SET 300
B → SET 500
Final result:
balance = 500
But logically:
1000 - 700 - 500 = -200
The system accepted withdrawals totaling:
1200
from an account containing:
1000
That's a race condition.
________________________________________
12.2 Why Redis itself isn't the problem
Redis executes individual commands atomically.
For example:
GET
SET
INCR
DECR
Each individual command runs without another Redis command being interleaved inside that command.
But your business operation is:
GET
 ↓
check
 ↓
SET
That's three operations.
Redis doesn't automatically understand that these three commands form one business transaction.
This distinction is critical:
Atomic command ≠ atomic business operation.
________________________________________
12.3 Redis MULTI / EXEC
Redis provides transactions through:
MULTI
EXEC
Conceptually:
MULTI
   command 1
   command 2
   command 3
EXEC
The commands are queued and then executed sequentially when EXEC happens.
Example:
MULTI
SET name "John"
SET age 30
INCR loginCount
EXEC
The key idea:
MULTI
 ↓
queue commands
 ↓
EXEC
 ↓
execute queued commands sequentially
________________________________________
12.4 But here's the trap
Coming from a relational database background, you might think:
Redis MULTI/EXEC
=
SQL transaction
No.
This is one of the most important things to understand.
Redis transactions do not provide the same semantics as a typical database transaction with:
BEGIN
...
ROLLBACK
COMMIT
Redis doesn't generally roll back previously executed commands if a later command fails.
For example:
MULTI

SET user:name "Alex"

INVALID_COMMAND

SET user:age 30

EXEC
The commands are queued, and execution semantics are different from a database transaction that can roll back the whole transaction.
Think of Redis transactions more as:
"Execute this group of commands sequentially without interleaving commands from other clients."
Not:
"Give me full ACID transaction semantics."
________________________________________
12.5 Why MULTI alone doesn't solve our balance problem
Remember:
balance = 1000
Request A wants:
withdraw 700
Request B wants:
withdraw 500
Suppose Java does:
GET balance
then calculates:
newBalance = balance - amount
and then sends:
MULTI
SET balance newBalance
EXEC
Still broken.
Why?
Because the problematic part happened before MULTI.
Timeline:
A: GET → 1000
B: GET → 1000

A: MULTI / SET 300 / EXEC
B: MULTI / SET 500 / EXEC
The transactions themselves execute atomically, but they don't know that the balance changed between the original reads.
So we need:
WATCH
________________________________________
12.6 WATCH = optimistic concurrency control
WATCH lets Redis detect whether watched keys changed before EXEC.
Think of it like:
"I'm going to make a decision based on this key. If somebody changes it before I commit my changes, abort my transaction."
Example:
WATCH balance:1001

GET balance:1001

MULTI
SET balance:300
EXEC
The important relationship is:
WATCH
  ↓
read
  ↓
calculate
  ↓
MULTI
  ↓
write
  ↓
EXEC
If another client changes the watched key before EXEC, the transaction is aborted.
________________________________________
12.7 Let's replay the withdrawal example
Initial:
balance = 1000
Request A
WATCH balance
GET balance
Gets:
1000
Request A calculates:
1000 - 700 = 300
But before A executes:
Request B
WATCH balance
GET balance
Gets:
1000
B calculates:
1000 - 500 = 500
Suppose A executes first:
MULTI
SET balance 300
EXEC
Now Redis knows:
balance changed
When B tries:
EXEC
Redis sees that its watched key changed.
Therefore:
EXEC
 ↓
aborted
B must retry the whole operation.
________________________________________
12.8 The Java retry loop
Conceptually your Java code becomes:
while (true) {

    redis.watch("balance:1001");

    int balance = redis.get("balance:1001");

    if (balance < amount) {
        redis.unwatch();
        return false;
    }

    int newBalance = balance - amount;

    redis.multi();
    redis.set("balance:1001", newBalance);

    if (redis.exec() != null) {
        return true;
    }

    // Someone modified the balance.
    // Retry.
}
This is optimistic locking.
You don't lock the key.
You optimistically assume:
"Probably nobody changes it."
If someone does:
EXEC fails
and you retry.
________________________________________
12.9 WATCH is conceptually similar to database optimistic locking
If you've used JPA, you may have seen:
version = 17
Application reads:
balance = 1000
version = 17
Then tries:
UPDATE account
SET balance = 300,
    version = 18
WHERE id = 1001
AND version = 17
If another transaction already changed version:
version != 17
the update doesn't happen.
Redis WATCH follows a similar optimistic-concurrency idea.
Conceptually:
Database:
version check

Redis:
WATCH
Not identical internally, but the reasoning model is similar.
________________________________________
12.10 When WATCH is a good solution
Imagine:
100 requests/sec
for the same key.
Most requests don't collide.
Then optimistic concurrency can work nicely:
Request
 ↓
WATCH
 ↓
read
 ↓
calculate
 ↓
EXEC
Most transactions succeed on the first attempt.
But now imagine:
100,000 requests/sec
all modifying the same key.
You'll get lots of:
WATCH
 ↓
conflict
 ↓
retry
 ↓
conflict
 ↓
retry
This can become ugly.
That's when we should ask:
"Why am I bringing the state into Java at all?"
And that leads to:
Lua scripting
________________________________________
12.11 The key idea behind Lua
Instead of:
Redis
 ↓
GET
 ↓
Java
 ↓
calculate
 ↓
Redis
 ↓
SET
we can execute the logic inside Redis.
Conceptually:
Java
 |
 | execute script
 v
Redis
 |
 | GET
 | check
 | calculate
 | SET
 |
 v
result
No other Redis command can interleave with the script execution.
That's the major advantage.
________________________________________
12.12 Our withdrawal example using Lua
We want Redis to perform:
balance = GET key

if balance >= amount:
    balance = balance - amount
    SET key balance
    return success

else:
    return failure
A Lua script could look conceptually like:
local balance = tonumber(redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]))
local amount = tonumber(ARGV[1])

if balance < amount then
    return 0
end

redis.call('SET', KEYS[1], balance - amount)

return 1
Java sends:
KEYS:
    balance:1001

ARGV:
    700
Redis executes the entire script atomically.
________________________________________
12.13 Timeline with Lua
Two requests:
A → withdraw 700
B → withdraw 500
Redis starts A's script:
balance = 1000

check:
1000 >= 700

SET:
300
Script finishes.
Then B executes:
balance = 300

check:
300 >= 500

false
B fails.
Final:
balance = 300
Correct.
No race.
No WATCH retry loop.
________________________________________
12.14 Why Lua works
Redis processes commands sequentially.
A Lua script executes as one Redis operation from the perspective of other commands.
Conceptually:
Client A
   |
   | Lua script
   v
Redis
   |
   +---------------------+
   | GET                 |
   | check               |
   | SET                 |
   +---------------------+
             |
             v
        script done
             |
             v
       Client B command
B cannot sneak another command between:
GET
and:
SET
inside the script.
That's the atomicity we wanted.
________________________________________
12.15 MULTI vs WATCH vs Lua
This is the decision framework I want you to remember.
MULTI/EXEC
Use when:
I already know the commands I want to execute.
Example:
MULTI
SET A ...
SET B ...
INCR C
EXEC
________________________________________
WATCH
Use when:
My decision depends on a value I read first,
and I want optimistic concurrency control.
Pattern:
WATCH
 ↓
READ
 ↓
DECIDE
 ↓
MULTI
 ↓
EXEC
________________________________________
Lua
Use when:
The business logic itself should execute atomically
inside Redis.
Pattern:
Java
 ↓
Lua script
 ↓
Redis:
  read
  validate
  calculate
  write
________________________________________
12.16 A practical comparison
	MULTI/EXEC	WATCH	Lua
Groups commands	Yes	Yes	Yes
Detects concurrent changes	No	Yes	Not needed
Server-side logic	No	No	Yes
Retry required	Usually no	Often	Usually no
Good for conditional logic	Limited	Yes	Excellent
Java round trips	Multiple/queued	Multiple	Usually one request
Atomic execution	Yes	Yes if EXEC succeeds	Yes
________________________________________
12.17 The round-trip difference matters
Suppose Java performs:
GET
Then:
SET
That's at least two client/server interactions.
With WATCH:
WATCH
GET
MULTI
SET
EXEC
There can be several interactions depending on the client API.
With Lua:
EVAL script
Conceptually:
Java
 |
 | one request
 v
Redis
 |
 | entire operation
 v
result
For high-latency environments, reducing round trips can matter significantly.
________________________________________
12.18 But Lua has an important limitation
A Lua script runs on the Redis server.
That means:
Don't put expensive computation inside Redis.
Bad idea:
Lua
 ↓
huge loop
 ↓
millions of iterations
 ↓
complex computation
Why?
Redis processes commands sequentially.
If your script blocks Redis for too long:
Request A
   |
   v
Long Lua script
   |
   X
Other requests wait
You've created a Redis-side bottleneck.
So Lua should generally be:
small
deterministic
bounded
fast
________________________________________
12.19 This is an important system-design tradeoff
You have three places where logic could happen:
                Business logic

       Java          Redis           DB
        |              |              |
        |              |              |
    flexible       atomic-ish      transactional
Putting logic into Java:
+ easy to maintain
+ rich language
- more network round trips
- concurrency problems
Putting small atomic logic into Redis Lua:
+ atomic
+ fewer round trips
+ close to data
- Redis-specific
- must keep scripts fast
- more complexity to test/version
Putting durable business state in DB:
+ strong transactional semantics
+ durable
+ rich constraints
- potentially slower/higher latency
- different scaling characteristics
The correct choice depends on what Redis represents in your architecture.
________________________________________
12.20 Cluster complication: multi-key operations
Now connect this to the previous lesson.
Suppose your Lua script uses:
KEYS[1] = user:{123}:balance
KEYS[2] = user:{123}:ledger
Because both have:
{123}
they map to the same hash slot.
That's good.
But if you have:
user:{123}:balance
user:{456}:balance
they probably map to different slots.
A Redis Cluster cannot simply execute an arbitrary multi-key atomic operation across different primaries.
Why?
Because Redis would need distributed transaction coordination across:
P1
 |
network
 |
P2
That is a much harder distributed-systems problem.
So Redis Cluster's multi-key design strongly encourages:
Keep keys participating in an atomic operation in the same hash slot.
This connects directly to hash tags from Topic 11.
________________________________________
12.21 Example: shopping cart reservation
Imagine an e-commerce system.
We have:
stock:{product123}
cart:{user456}
Suppose you want one atomic operation:
decrease stock
add item to cart
If these keys hash to different slots:
stock:{product123} → P1

cart:{user456} → P2
you cannot simply make this one cluster-wide atomic Lua transaction.
You need to rethink the data model or the consistency boundary.
This is a system-design decision, not merely a Redis API limitation.
________________________________________
12.22 A better design might be
If the atomic operation is:
"Modify all state associated with one order"
you might design:
order:{123}:status
order:{123}:items
order:{123}:reservation
Now:
{123}
becomes the consistency boundary.
All related keys go to one slot.
This is an extremely useful design principle:
Choose your Redis hash tag around the entity that needs atomic operations.
But don't blindly use a single tag for everything.
Because then you can create:
hot entity
   ↓
hot slot
   ↓
hot primary
________________________________________
12.23 Lua and idempotency
Here's another production-level issue.
Suppose Java calls:
EVAL withdrawalScript
but the network response is lost.
Java doesn't know:
Did the script execute?
If Java retries the script blindly:
execute
↓
response lost
↓
retry
the operation might happen twice.
Lua gives you atomicity, but not automatically exactly-once semantics across a network failure.
That's a very important distinction.
You may need an idempotency key:
withdrawal:{requestId}
Then the script can conceptually check:
Has requestId already been processed?
       |
      yes → return previous result
       |
       no → perform operation + record requestId
Now your application can safely retry.
This is the kind of pattern that becomes important in real production systems.
________________________________________
12.24 The hierarchy you should now see
There are several different problems:
Problem 1 — Atomic command
INCR
Redis already handles it.
Problem 2 — Multiple commands must execute together
MULTI / EXEC
Problem 3 — Decision depends on current state
WATCH + MULTI/EXEC
Problem 4 — Conditional logic should happen atomically
Lua
Problem 5 — Client may retry after ambiguous network failure
Idempotency
These are different problems.
Don't use:
"Lua"
as a magic answer to everything.
________________________________________
12.25 Final mental model
                  Redis concurrency

                         |
          +--------------+--------------+
          |              |              |
       Simple          Grouped       Conditional
       command         commands        logic
          |              |              |
        INCR         MULTI/EXEC        Lua
                                         
                         |
                   Optimistic
                   concurrency
                         |
                       WATCH
And for Cluster:
Java
 |
 v
Redis Cluster
 |
 +---- slot A ----> P1
 |
 +---- slot B ----> P2
 |
 +---- slot C ----> P3
If atomic logic needs multiple keys:
key A ──┐
        ├── same hash slot → same primary
key B ──┘
Use:
{entityId}
when appropriate.
________________________________________
The 5 things I want you to be able to explain
Without looking anything up, you should now be able to answer:
1.	Why doesn't GET → Java calculation → SET guarantee correctness? 
2.	Why doesn't MULTI/EXEC alone solve optimistic concurrency? 
3.	What does WATCH actually protect against? 
4.	Why can Lua solve the withdrawal race without WATCH? 
5.	Why do Redis Cluster multi-key atomic operations create a hash-slot constraint? 
If you can explain those five, you've understood the topic rather than memorized Redis commands.

13. Redis Performance + Memory
This topic is where Redis starts becoming production engineering rather than just a data structure.
A common mistake is to think:
"Redis is in-memory, therefore it is always fast."
Not necessarily.
You can have:
Redis CPU = 35%
Memory = 60%
and still experience:
P99 latency = 150 ms
The interesting question is why.
We'll build the answer from the Redis execution model.
________________________________________
13.1 First: what actually makes Redis fast?
The basic architecture is:
Java Application
       |
       | TCP
       v
+--------------------+
|       Redis        |
|                    |
| Event loop         |
|      ↓             |
| Command execution  |
|      ↓             |
| In-memory data     |
+--------------------+
The important property historically associated with Redis is:
Redis executes commands without needing a thread per command.
Modern Redis can use threads for some networking/I/O work, but the core command execution model remains predominantly single-threaded.
That design has a huge advantage:
No lock
No context switching between command-execution threads
No concurrent mutation of the same data structure
So a simple command such as:
GET user:123
can be extremely fast.
________________________________________
13.2 Why doesn't Redis use 32 threads for commands?
Suppose 32 threads simultaneously modify:
HashMap
Sorted Set
List
You now need synchronization:
Thread 1 ──┐
Thread 2 ──┤
Thread 3 ──┤
            ├── shared data
Thread 4 ──┤
Thread 5 ──┘
That introduces:
locks
contention
cache coherence
context switching
Redis instead historically chose:
              Redis command execution

GET ──────┐
SET ──────┤
INCR ─────┤──→ Event loop → execute sequentially
HSET ─────┤
LPUSH ────┘
This makes individual operations very predictable.
But it creates a critical consequence:
One expensive command can delay everybody else.
This is one of the most important Redis performance concepts.
________________________________________
13.3 The "CPU only 40%, but Redis is slow" problem
Suppose your Redis has:
8 CPU cores
and Redis command execution is primarily using one core.
You observe:
overall CPU = 12.5%
You might think:
"Redis has plenty of CPU available."
But imagine the Redis command-processing core is saturated:
Core 1 → 100%
Core 2 → 0%
Core 3 → 0%
...
Core 8 → 0%
Overall:
~12.5%
Yet Redis can be completely bottlenecked.
So:
Machine-wide CPU percentage can be misleading for Redis.
You need to understand where the bottleneck is, not merely total CPU.
________________________________________
13.4 Now imagine a BIG command
Suppose somebody executes:
LRANGE huge-list 0 -1
and the list contains:
10 million elements
Redis has to process a huge amount of data.
While that work is happening:
Request A → huge LRANGE
             ↓
          Redis busy
             ↓
Request B → GET
Request C → SET
Request D → INCR
B, C and D can experience latency.
This is why:
A single slow Redis command can affect unrelated requests.
________________________________________
13.5 Big keys
This leads to one of the most common Redis production problems:
Big Keys
Imagine:
user:123
contains:
500 MB
Maybe it's:
Hash
List
Set
Sorted Set
String
A big key creates several problems.
Memory
Obviously:
500 MB
is significant.
Network
If you retrieve it:
GET huge-value
you have to transfer:
500 MB
over the network.
CPU
Serialization/deserialization and command processing can become expensive.
Latency
A large operation can block other Redis work.
Replication
That data may need to propagate to replicas.
Persistence
Depending on configuration, large datasets can also affect RDB/AOF behavior.
So:
Big keys are not merely a memory problem. They are a latency + network + replication + operational problem.
________________________________________
13.6 Example: Java application
Suppose:
String session = redis.get("session:123");
Usually fine.
But somebody stores:
session:123 = 20 MB JSON
Now every request does:
Redis
  ↓
20 MB
  ↓
Network
  ↓
JVM
  ↓
String
  ↓
JSON parsing
Your Redis might be fast internally, but the entire request path isn't.
Remember the system-level latency:
Total latency ≈

Redis processing
+ network
+ serialization
+ deserialization
+ JVM processing
________________________________________
13.7 Network latency matters enormously
Suppose Redis command execution takes:
0.1 ms
but your Java service and Redis are separated by:
network RTT = 1 ms
A single operation might be around:
~1 ms
not:
~0.1 ms
Now suppose your code performs:
GET
GET
GET
GET
GET
sequentially.
You can end up paying:
1 ms × 5 = ~5 ms
just in network round trips.
This leads us to:
Pipelining
________________________________________
13.8 Pipelining
Without pipelining:
Java → GET → Redis
Java ← result

Java → GET → Redis
Java ← result

Java → GET → Redis
Java ← result
Lots of waiting.
With pipelining:
Java
 |
 +── GET A ──────┐
 +── GET B ──────┤
 +── GET C ──────┤──→ Redis
 +── GET D ──────┘
                  |
             execute/process
                  |
        ← results ────────
The application sends multiple commands without waiting for each response.
This dramatically reduces network round-trip overhead.
________________________________________
13.9 Pipeline ≠ transaction
Very important.
People often confuse:
pipeline
with:
MULTI/EXEC
They are different.
Pipeline
Primarily about:
network efficiency
MULTI/EXEC
About:
transactional command grouping / atomic execution
You can think:
Pipeline:
"Send many things efficiently."


Transaction:
"Execute this group as a Redis transaction."
They solve different problems.
________________________________________
13.10 Pipeline example
Suppose your Java service needs:
GET user:123
GET user:123:cart
GET user:123:preferences
GET user:123:permissions
Sequential:
4 requests
4 responses
4 network waits
Pipeline:
4 commands
      ↓
one batch
      ↓
Redis
      ↓
4 responses
This can dramatically improve throughput when network latency is significant.
But don't blindly create enormous pipelines.
________________________________________
13.11 Huge pipelines can also hurt
Imagine:
Pipeline = 100,000 commands
Now:
Java
 ↓
build huge batch
 ↓
send huge payload
 ↓
Redis processes huge batch
 ↓
huge response
You may create:
memory pressure
latency spikes
large network packets
long event-loop work
So the correct strategy is usually bounded batching.
For example conceptually:
100–1000 commands/batch
rather than:
10 million commands in one pipeline
The optimal size depends on your workload.
________________________________________
13.12 MGET vs multiple GETs
Suppose all keys are compatible with your topology.
Instead of:
GET A
GET B
GET C
GET D
you can sometimes use:
MGET A B C D
That's another way to reduce overhead.
But now remember Topic 11.
In Redis Cluster:
A → slot 100
B → slot 2000
C → slot 9000
D → slot 14000
These keys belong to different nodes.
A cluster cannot treat arbitrary cross-slot multi-key operations as one normal atomic operation.
So cluster-aware clients may need to split operations or the command may be rejected depending on the API/operation.
This is why key design and performance are connected.
________________________________________
13.13 Hash tags can improve locality
Suppose:
user:{123}:profile
user:{123}:cart
user:{123}:permissions
All share:
{123}
Therefore they map to the same slot.
Now operations involving those keys have locality.
But there's a tradeoff.
If one user becomes extremely hot:
user:{123}
all that traffic can hit one primary.
So:
Hash tag
   ↓
better locality
   ↓
multi-key compatibility

BUT

hot entity
   ↓
hot slot
   ↓
hot primary
Performance design is always about tradeoffs.
________________________________________
13.14 Hot Keys
Now let's distinguish:
Big Key
One key contains too much data.
product:123 → 500 MB
Hot Key
One key receives too much traffic.
product:123
   ↓
500,000 requests/sec
It might be tiny:
product:123 = "iPhone"
but extremely popular.
So:
Big key
=
large data per key

Hot key
=
high request frequency per key
They are different problems.
________________________________________
13.15 Why hot keys are dangerous in Redis Cluster
Suppose:
Redis Cluster

P1
P2
P3
P4
P5
P6
Traffic:
99% → product:123
If:
product:123 → slot 7421 → P3
then:
P1 → low traffic
P2 → low traffic
P3 → 99% traffic
P4 → low traffic
P5 → low traffic
P6 → low traffic
Adding more Redis nodes won't necessarily help.
Why?
Because the hot key belongs to one slot.
That slot belongs to one primary.
________________________________________
13.16 How can you solve a hot key?
One strategy is replication/read distribution, depending on the access pattern and client capabilities.
Another is application-level key replication.
Instead of:
product:123
you could conceptually have:
product:123:replica:1
product:123:replica:2
product:123:replica:3
...
and distribute reads.
But now you have a consistency problem.
If product data changes:
product:123 = new price
you need to propagate the update.
So:
Hot key solution
        ↓
more read distribution
        ↓
more copies
        ↓
more consistency complexity
Again: tradeoff.
________________________________________
13.17 Memory: Redis stores more than your values
This is another common beginner mistake.
Suppose you calculate:
10 million users × 100 bytes
=
1 GB
You might assume Redis needs approximately:
1 GB
Not necessarily.
Redis memory includes overhead for:
keys
values
hash tables
pointers
object metadata
allocator overhead
data structures
replication buffers
client buffers
temporary memory
etc.
So:
logical dataset size
        ≠
actual Redis RSS
This matters enormously when sizing production Redis.
________________________________________
13.18 Example
Suppose:
10 million keys
and each value is only:
50 bytes
Raw values:
500 MB
But 10 million keys themselves require memory.
Plus:
Redis data structure overhead
+
allocator overhead
+
key strings
+
metadata
You could end up requiring substantially more than 500 MB.
Therefore:
Never size Redis purely by adding the payload sizes.
Measure realistic memory usage.
________________________________________
13.19 Redis data structures affect memory
Consider:
String
Hash
List
Set
Sorted Set
They don't all have the same overhead.
For example, a million tiny independent keys can have considerably more metadata overhead than a carefully modeled compact structure.
This creates a classic tradeoff.
You might have:
1,000,000 keys
versus:
10,000 hashes
with many fields inside them.
But don't optimize purely for memory.
You must consider:
access pattern
update pattern
expiration
atomic operations
hotness
cluster distribution
________________________________________
13.20 TTL creates another memory dimension
Suppose your cache contains:
user:1
user:2
...
user:10M
with:
TTL = 30 minutes
This is good because stale entries eventually disappear.
But imagine your application suddenly stops reading/writing many keys.
Expiration behavior matters.
Redis has mechanisms to remove expired keys rather than needing to synchronously scan the entire database for every expiration.
The important conceptual model is:
TTL
 ↓
key becomes logically expired
 ↓
Redis removes expired data
This is one reason you shouldn't assume:
"At exactly T+30 minutes every key physically disappears in one giant operation."
Expiration is managed incrementally.
________________________________________
13.21 Eviction
Now suppose Redis reaches its configured memory limit.
What happens?
Depending on configuration, Redis can use an eviction policy.
Conceptually:
Memory limit reached
       ↓
choose candidate keys
       ↓
evict
       ↓
make room
Common policy families include:
noeviction
allkeys-lru
allkeys-lfu
volatile-lru
volatile-lfu
allkeys-random
volatile-random
volatile-ttl
You don't need to memorize every policy yet.
Understand the distinction:
allkeys
Candidates can come from:
all keys
volatile
Candidates are restricted to keys with:
TTL
This matters enormously for cache architecture.
________________________________________
13.22 LRU vs LFU
Two important concepts:
LRU
Least Recently Used.
Idea:
Evict things that haven't been accessed recently.
Good when:
recently accessed ≈ likely to be accessed again
LFU
Least Frequently Used.
Idea:
Evict things that are rarely accessed.
Useful when a small set of objects are consistently popular.
Example:
Product A → 1,000,000 accesses
Product B → 2 accesses
LFU recognizes A as hot.
________________________________________
13.23 Cache architecture decision
Suppose Redis is purely a cache:
Application
    |
    v
Redis
    |
 cache miss
    v
Database
Eviction can be perfectly normal.
For example:
memory full
 ↓
evict cold cache entry
 ↓
next request
 ↓
cache miss
 ↓
DB
 ↓
repopulate
But if Redis is holding critical primary state:
memory full
 ↓
eviction
 ↓
data disappears
that's a completely different situation.
Therefore:
Eviction policy depends on whether Redis is a cache or a source of truth.
________________________________________
13.24 Memory fragmentation
Now something more subtle.
Suppose Redis logically stores:
10 GB
but the operating system reports:
RSS = 14 GB
Why?
One possible reason is memory fragmentation.
Conceptually:
Redis allocator
     |
     +── allocated blocks
     +── freed blocks
     +── differently sized blocks
Memory can become less efficiently packed.
So:
used_memory
and:
used_memory_rss
can differ.
Think:
used_memory
=
Redis's view of allocated data

RSS
=
memory actually resident from the OS perspective
A large difference can indicate fragmentation or other process memory effects.
________________________________________
13.25 Why fragmentation matters
Imagine:
used_memory = 8 GB
RSS         = 12 GB
You have:
4 GB difference
That can cause operational problems.
Your machine may look like:
RAM = 16 GB
Redis RSS = 12 GB
Other processes = 3 GB
You're already near the edge.
Then a temporary memory spike occurs.
Potentially:
OOM
So production Redis sizing needs headroom.
Don't run:
Redis maxmemory = 95% of physical RAM
without understanding the surrounding memory requirements and operational behavior.
________________________________________
13.26 Persistence can create memory pressure
Now add persistence.
Redis may use:
RDB
AOF
depending on configuration.
During certain persistence operations, Redis can temporarily require additional memory, particularly due to copy-on-write behavior when creating a background snapshot.
Conceptually:
Redis process
     |
     +---- main dataset
     |
     +---- fork/background persistence
                  |
                  ↓
            copy-on-write pages
If lots of memory pages change while the background operation runs, additional memory can be consumed.
So:
dataset fits in RAM
doesn't automatically mean:
production system fits safely in RAM
You need headroom for:
replication
buffers
persistence
fork/COW effects
clients
allocator fragmentation
________________________________________
13.27 Replication also consumes memory
Suppose:
P1
 |
 R1
Redis needs replication buffers and network resources.
If a replica falls behind, the primary may need to maintain enough replication state to help it catch up, depending on the replication mechanism and configuration.
So your memory model isn't:
dataset only
It's closer to:
Redis memory requirement ≈

dataset
+ key/value overhead
+ allocator overhead
+ client buffers
+ replication buffers
+ persistence overhead
+ temporary operations
+ safety headroom
That's a much better production mental model.
________________________________________
13.28 Serialization matters in Java
Redis may be fast, but your Java application can still waste time.
For example:
Redis
 ↓
JSON string
 ↓
Jackson
 ↓
Java object
Suppose you're storing:
{
  "id": 123,
  "name": "...",
  "preferences": "...",
  ...
}
Every request may involve:
network
+
JSON parsing
+
object allocation
+
GC
At high throughput, JVM allocation and garbage collection can become significant.
So when investigating latency:
Redis slow?
is not enough.
Check:
Redis latency
network latency
serialization
JVM CPU
JVM GC
thread pools
connection pool
________________________________________
13.29 Connection pooling
Java applications commonly use a Redis client with connection management.
Suppose you have:
200 application threads
and create:
200 Redis connections
That isn't automatically optimal.
Too few connections:
threads
  ↓
wait for connection
  ↓
latency
Too many:
many connections
 ↓
Redis client overhead
 ↓
server connection overhead
 ↓
network/resource pressure
So connection pool sizing is another part of performance engineering.
Don't think:
"More connections = more performance."
There is a saturation point.
________________________________________
13.30 A very useful performance equation
At a high level:
Throughput ≈ work / time
But for a Redis request, latency comes from multiple components:
Latency ≈

queueing
+ network
+ Redis command execution
+ serialization/deserialization
+ application processing
For example:
Network          0.8 ms
Redis            0.1 ms
JSON             0.3 ms
JVM processing   0.4 ms
-----------------------
Total            1.6 ms
Optimizing Redis from:
0.1 ms → 0.05 ms
only saves:
0.05 ms
while optimizing five unnecessary network round trips might save much more.
This is why profiling beats guessing.
________________________________________
13.31 The latency percentile problem
Never evaluate Redis performance using only:
average latency
Suppose:
Requests = 1,000,000

Average = 2 ms
P50     = 1 ms
P95     = 3 ms
P99     = 20 ms
P99.9   = 300 ms
Your average looks excellent.
But 0.1% of:
1,000,000
is:
1,000 requests
experiencing terrible latency.
For production systems, you care heavily about:
P95
P99
P99.9
depending on your SLO.
________________________________________
13.32 Why P99 suddenly explodes
Imagine Redis normally processes:
GET → 0.2 ms
Then somebody executes:
SMEMBERS giant-set
or:
LRANGE giant-list 0 -1
Redis spends a long time processing it.
During that period:
normal GET
      ↓
waiting
So you might see:
P50 = 0.3 ms
P95 = 1 ms
P99 = 50 ms
This is why tail latency is strongly affected by expensive commands.
________________________________________
13.33 Command complexity matters
You should have some intuition for Redis command complexity.
For example:
GET
SET
HGET
are generally expected to be very fast.
But operations involving many elements can scale with data size.
Conceptually:
O(1)
versus:
O(N)
If:
N = 10
no big deal.
If:
N = 10,000,000
very different story.
Therefore:
Never look only at the command name. Look at the size of the data structure involved.
________________________________________
13.34 A production debugging scenario
Suppose your Spring Boot service reports:
Redis P99:
2 ms → 120 ms
You investigate.
Step 1
Check Redis command latency.
Maybe:
Redis itself = 1 ms
Then Redis may not be the bottleneck.
Step 2
Check network.
Maybe:
network RTT = 80 ms
Problem found.
Or perhaps:
network = 1 ms
Step 3
Check slow/expensive commands.
You discover:
LRANGE huge-list
Step 4
Check big keys.
Find:
session:123 = 50 MB
Step 5
Check JVM.
Maybe:
GC pauses = 80 ms
Redis is innocent.
This is how you should debug distributed systems:
Follow the request path rather than blaming the obvious component.
________________________________________
13.35 Your production Redis performance checklist
Think in these dimensions:
                    Redis Performance
                           |
       +-------------------+-------------------+
       |                   |                   |
    Command             Network             Memory
       |                   |                   |
   complexity          RTT                 big keys
   expensive ops       pipelines           fragmentation
   Lua scripts         batching             eviction
       |                   |                   |
       +-------------------+-------------------+
                           |
                         Java
                           |
                  serialization
                  connection pool
                  GC
                  thread contention
And at the cluster level:
                 Redis Cluster
                      |
       +--------------+--------------+
       |              |              |
    Hot key        Hot slot      Uneven slots
       |              |              |
   one key        one primary    imbalance
________________________________________
13.36 What I want you to remember
If you remember only these 10 points, you've got the important part:
1. Redis command execution is predominantly single-threaded
So one expensive operation can impact others.
2. CPU percentage can mislead you
One busy Redis execution core can exist while overall machine CPU looks low.
3. Big key ≠ hot key
Big key → large data
Hot key → high traffic
4. Pipelining solves network round-trip overhead
Not transaction semantics.
5. MGET/multi-key operations have Cluster constraints
Hash slots matter.
6. Redis memory ≠ just payload size
Account for overhead and operational memory.
7. used_memory ≠ necessarily RSS
Fragmentation and other memory consumers matter.
8. Persistence/replication can require additional memory
Always leave headroom.
9. Lua can improve atomicity and reduce round trips
But a long Lua script can hurt the entire Redis instance.
10. Measure P99, not just average
Tail latency is where production incidents often hide.
