                              |
          +-------------------+-------------------+
          |                   |                   |
         P1                  P2                  P3
          |                   |                   |
      slots A             slots B             slots C
          |                   |                   |
      memory              memory              memory
          |                   |                   |
      replicas            replicas            replicas
When performance goes bad, investigate each layer, not just Redis.
14. Redis Failure Modes
This is one of the most important topics in the entire Redis series.
Knowing Redis commands is easy.
The real production question is:
"What does my Java application do when Redis is slow, unavailable, inconsistent, or partially failed?"
A strong system designer doesn't design only the happy path:
Java → Redis → response
They design:
Java → Redis → ?
             ↓
          timeout
          node down
          network partition
          stale replica
          overload
          memory pressure
          failover
Let's reason through these one by one.
________________________________________
14.1 First principle: Redis is not one failure mode
When someone says:
"Redis is down."
That statement is too vague.
There are many different situations:
                    Redis failure
                         |
       +-----------------+------------------+
       |                 |                  |
    Hard down         Slow              Partial
       |                 |                  |
   connection        timeout           one node
   refused           latency            failed
And they require different responses.
For example:
Redis completely unavailable
might be easy to detect.
But:
Redis responds in 10 seconds
can be much more dangerous.
Why?
Because your application threads may sit there waiting.
________________________________________
14.2 Failure mode #1 — Redis completely unavailable
Normal:
Java
 |
 | GET user:123
 v
Redis
 |
 v
"value"
Failure:
Java
 |
 | GET user:123
 X
Redis unavailable
Your Java client may experience:
connection refused
connection reset
timeout
I/O exception
Now the first system-design question:
Is Redis a cache or the source of truth?
This completely changes the architecture.
________________________________________
14.3 Redis as a cache
Typical architecture:
                +--------+
Java ──────────→| Redis  |
  |             +--------+
  |
  | cache miss / Redis failure
  v
+----------+
| Database |
+----------+
If Redis fails:
Redis X
  |
  v
Database
The application can potentially continue.
Example:
GET user:123

Redis unavailable
       ↓
fallback to DB
       ↓
return user
This is called fail-open-ish cache behavior:
Cache failure should not necessarily become application failure.
But there is a massive trap.
________________________________________
14.4 The database can suddenly become the victim
Imagine:
Normal traffic:

1,000 req/sec

Redis:
900 cache hits
100 DB requests
Now Redis fails.
Suddenly:
1,000 req/sec
        ↓
Database
Your DB traffic increased:
100 → 1,000 req/sec
That's a 10× increase.
If Redis was handling:
100K req/sec
and your database normally receives:
5K req/sec
Redis failure could potentially push:
5K → 100K
toward the database.
The database may collapse.
Then:
Redis failure
     ↓
DB overload
     ↓
DB latency
     ↓
Java thread pool exhaustion
     ↓
application failure
This is a classic cascading failure.
________________________________________
14.5 The cache failure cascade
Visualize it:
                Redis
                  X
                  |
                  v
              Cache miss
                  |
                  v
               Database
                  |
              overloaded
                  |
                  v
              slow queries
                  |
                  v
          Java threads blocked
                  |
                  v
             API failure
So:
A cache should protect the database, but a cache outage can destroy the database if fallback isn't controlled.
This is one of the most important Redis production lessons.
________________________________________
14.6 Failure mode #2 — Redis is slow, not down
This is arguably more dangerous.
Suppose:
Redis latency = 2 ms
normally.
Suddenly:
Redis latency = 5 seconds
Redis isn't technically down.
Your application still does:
GET
 ↓
wait
 ↓
wait
 ↓
wait
Suppose you have:
Tomcat threads = 200
and all 200 threads are waiting on Redis.
Then:
200 threads
   ↓
blocked
   ↓
no capacity for new requests
Your API can become unavailable even though:
Redis is technically "up"
This is why:
Availability is not binary.
A dependency can be alive but unusable.
________________________________________
14.7 Timeouts are mandatory
Never let a remote dependency wait indefinitely.
Conceptually:
Java
 |
 | GET
 v
Redis
 |
 | no response
 |
