 |
 X
The application regains control.
Without a timeout:
Redis slow
   ↓
Java waits
   ↓
threads accumulate
   ↓
thread pool exhausted
   ↓
service unavailable
Timeouts are a resource-protection mechanism, not just a networking configuration.
________________________________________
14.8 But timeout too low?
Suppose Redis normally has:
P99 = 3 ms
You configure:
timeout = 1 ms
Now legitimate requests can timeout.
You might create:
false failures
So timeout selection should consider:
normal latency
P99/P99.9
network latency
GC pauses
failover behavior
SLO
You don't want:
timeout = huge
or:
timeout = unrealistically tiny
________________________________________
14.9 Failure mode #3 — Retry storm
Suppose Redis times out.
Your application says:
"Let's retry!"
First request:
GET
 ↓
timeout
Retry:
GET
 ↓
timeout
Retry:
GET
 ↓
timeout
Now imagine:
10,000 application instances
all doing this.
You can get:
Redis struggling
      ↓
timeouts
      ↓
retries
      ↓
more traffic
      ↓
Redis struggles more
      ↓
more timeouts
This is a retry storm.
Retries can turn a temporary failure into a major outage.
________________________________________
14.10 Exponential backoff
Instead of:
retry immediately
retry immediately
retry immediately
use increasing delays:
attempt 1 → immediate
attempt 2 → 10 ms
attempt 3 → 20 ms
attempt 4 → 40 ms
attempt 5 → 80 ms
Conceptually:
delay = base × 2^attempt
Usually add jitter.
For example:
100 ms
± random amount
Why?
Suppose 10,000 clients all retry exactly at:
100 ms
Then you get another synchronized spike.
Jitter spreads them out:
Client A → 93 ms
Client B → 107 ms
Client C → 116 ms
Client D → 88 ms
...
This is much healthier.
________________________________________
14.11 Retry only when retry makes sense
This is critical.
Not every operation should be blindly retried.
Consider:
GET user:123
A retry is often reasonable.
But:
INCR balance
is different.
Suppose:
Java → INCR
Redis executes it.
But response is lost:
Redis → Java
          X
Java doesn't know whether Redis executed it.
If Java retries:
INCR
you may increment twice.
So:
A timeout creates ambiguity: did the operation happen or not?
This is why idempotency matters.
________________________________________
14.12 Idempotent vs non-idempotent operations
Conceptually:
Idempotent
Doing it multiple times produces the same final state.
SET user:123 status=ACTIVE
Repeated:
SET ACTIVE
SET ACTIVE
SET ACTIVE
Final state:
ACTIVE
Non-idempotent
Repeated execution changes the result.
INCR counter
One execution:
10 → 11
Two executions:
10 → 12
Therefore retry strategy should depend on operation semantics.
________________________________________
14.13 Better pattern: idempotency key
For important operations, use an operation identifier.
Example:
requestId = abc-123
Store:
operation:{abc-123}
Conceptually:
Request
  |
  v
Does requestId already exist?
  |
  +---- YES → return previous result
  |
  +---- NO
          |
          v
      perform operation
          |
          v
      record requestId
Now if the client retries:
same requestId
the system can recognize:
"I've already processed this."
This pattern is extremely useful for payment/order/inventory-like operations.
________________________________________
14.14 Failure mode #4 — Redis primary crashes
Now use the Cluster model:
P1       P2       P3
|        |        |
R1       R2       R3
P2 dies:
P1       X       P3
|                |
R1              R3

        R2
Redis Cluster can promote R2.
R2
 ↓
new primary
During this process there can be a period where requests for P2's slots fail or are redirected.
Your application therefore needs to tolerate:
connection errors
MOVED
ASK
temporary unavailable
retryable failures
Cluster-aware Redis clients handle much of the topology management.
But your business operation still needs appropriate retry semantics.
________________________________________
14.15 Failover doesn't mean zero downtime
A common misconception:
"We have replicas, therefore Redis has zero downtime."
No.
There is still a transition:
Primary failure
      ↓
failure detection
      ↓
election
      ↓
replica promotion
      ↓
topology update
      ↓
client reconnect/reroute
That takes time.
During that window:
some requests may fail
So replicas improve availability; they don't make failure invisible.
________________________________________
14.16 Replication lag
Now consider:
P1
 |
 | replication
 v
R1
P1 receives:
SET balance 100
But R1 hasn't applied it yet.
Then:
P1 X
R1 becomes primary.
R1 may contain:
balance = 90
instead of:
balance = 100
Potentially the latest acknowledged state isn't present on the promoted replica.
This creates a data-loss window.
How acceptable is that?
Depends on what Redis is doing.
Cache
Probably:
acceptable
because DB is authoritative.
Session
Maybe acceptable depending on requirements.
Critical financial state
Potentially unacceptable.
This is why architecture must define:
source of truth
durability requirements
consistency requirements
acceptable data loss
________________________________________
14.17 Failure mode #5 — Network partition
This is a much harder distributed-systems problem.
Imagine:
          Network partition

      P1 --------X-------- P2
P1 and P2 cannot communicate.
But both machines are alive.
From P1's perspective:
P2 is unreachable
From P2's perspective:
P1 is unreachable
This is not the same as:
machine crashed
The machine may be perfectly healthy.
The network path is broken.
________________________________________
14.18 Why network partitions are dangerous
Imagine:
Application A → P1

Application B → P2
If the cluster cannot correctly coordinate ownership, you risk conflicting views of the system.
Distributed systems have to answer:
"Who is allowed to become primary?"
and:
"Can this node safely continue accepting writes?"
This is where quorum and cluster failure rules matter.
Redis Cluster is designed to prevent arbitrary independent masters from safely operating as if they were the same cluster partition indefinitely.
________________________________________
14.19 Think in terms of authority
A useful mental model:
Who owns these slots?
        ↓
Which node is primary?
        ↓
Has that primary actually failed?
        ↓
Can another node take ownership?
The cluster must establish these facts through its distributed coordination.
This is why simply having replicas isn't enough.
________________________________________
14.20 Failure mode #6 — Memory exhaustion
Suppose:
Redis maxmemory = 16 GB
and usage reaches:
15.9 GB
Now behavior depends on configuration.
If configured for eviction:
new data
  ↓
evict eligible keys
  ↓
make room
If configured with noeviction:
write
 ↓
OOM-style command failure
For a cache, eviction may be expected.
For primary application state, this could be disastrous.
Again:
Memory policy must match data semantics.
________________________________________
14.21 Memory pressure can create latency problems before OOM
You don't necessarily go directly from:
healthy
to:
OOM
There can be intermediate effects:
high memory
 ↓
fragmentation
 ↓
allocation pressure
 ↓
persistence pressure
 ↓
latency
 ↓
timeouts
So memory monitoring isn't just about preventing crashes.
It's also about preventing latency degradation.
________________________________________
14.22 Failure mode #7 — Big key / expensive command
Suppose one application accidentally executes:
SMEMBERS giant-set
where:
N = 20 million
Redis can become busy processing the operation.
Meanwhile:
GET
GET
SET
INCR
wait.
The application starts reporting:
Redis latency ↑
Then:
timeouts ↑
Then:
retries ↑
Then:
load ↑
This can become a cascade.
So a performance bug can become an availability incident.
That's an important distributed-systems lesson.
________________________________________
14.23 Failure mode #8 — Thundering herd
This one is extremely important for caches.
Suppose:
Redis:
user:123 → value
TTL = 60 sec
10,000 requests/sec depend on it.
The key expires.
Now:
10,000 requests
       ↓
Redis MISS
       ↓
10,000 DB queries
Instead of:
1 DB query
you have:
10,000 DB queries
This is the thundering herd / cache stampede problem.
________________________________________
14.24 Preventing cache stampede
One strategy:
Request 1
   ↓
cache miss
   ↓
acquire lock
   ↓
DB
   ↓
populate cache
Other requests:
Request 2 ─┐
Request 3 ─┤
Request 4 ─┤→ wait / serve stale / retry
Request 5 ─┘
Only one request rebuilds the cache.
Another strategy:
Refresh before expiration.
Instead of allowing the most popular key to expire at exactly the same time for everyone, refresh it proactively.
Another technique:
TTL jitter
Instead of:
TTL = 60 sec
for every key:
TTL = 55–65 sec
approximately.
This avoids synchronized expiration.
________________________________________
14.25 Failure mode #9 — Cache avalanche
Imagine millions of keys are populated at:
10:00:00
and all receive:
TTL = 1 hour
Then around:
11:00:00
a huge number expire together.
You get:
millions of cache misses
        ↓
database overload
That's a cache avalanche.
TTL jitter helps:
key A → 3580 sec
key B → 3640 sec
key C → 3710 sec
...
instead of:
everything → 3600 sec
________________________________________
14.26 Failure mode #10 — Cache penetration
Another pattern:
GET user:999999999
doesn't exist.
Application:
Redis miss
 ↓
DB query
 ↓
not found
Attacker/client repeatedly asks:
user:999999999
user:999999998
user:999999997
...
Every request bypasses Redis and hits the DB.
You get:
Redis is healthy
DB is overloaded
This is cache penetration.
A common defense is caching negative results:
user:999999999 → NOT_FOUND
with a short TTL.
________________________________________
14.27 Failure mode #11 — Connection pool exhaustion
Suppose Java has:
Redis connection pool = 50
Redis becomes slow.
Requests hold connections longer:
Request 1 → connection
Request 2 → connection
...
Request 50 → connection
Now:
Request 51
   ↓
wait for connection
