replica status
offset differences
Cluster
slot coverage
node health
failovers
MOVED/ASK activity
Commands
slow commands
expensive commands
command distribution
________________________________________
16.26 The metrics I care about most
If I had to prioritize:
1. P99 latency
2. memory utilization
3. evictions
4. replication lag
5. ops/sec
6. connected clients
7. blocked clients
8. rejected connections/commands
9. failovers
10. slowlog / command latency
Because these tell us:
Can Redis serve traffic?
Can it fit the workload?
Are replicas healthy?
Is it approaching failure?
________________________________________
16.27 Monitor the Java side too
This is commonly forgotten.
Redis can look healthy while Java is failing.
Monitor:
Redis command latency
        +
connection pool wait time
        +
connection pool utilization
        +
command timeout rate
        +
retry count
        +
application thread pool
For example:
Redis P99 = 3 ms
but:
Java Redis pool wait = 200 ms
Your application still experiences:
203 ms
approximately.
The bottleneck might be the client pool rather than Redis itself.
________________________________________
16.28 Slow commands
Redis is extremely fast when commands are appropriate.
But an expensive command can hurt the whole instance.
For example:
huge collection
       ↓
expensive operation
       ↓
Redis event loop busy
       ↓
other commands wait
So production architecture includes command discipline.
Avoid blindly using operations that can process huge datasets in one synchronous step.
Design data structures around access patterns.
________________________________________
16.29 Key design matters
Bad:
user
Potentially huge shared object.
Better:
user:123
user:456
user:789
For distributed systems, key design also affects:
hotspots
cluster distribution
memory
operational debugging
TTL management
________________________________________
16.30 Hot keys in Redis Cluster
This is subtle.
Suppose:
product:viral
receives:
500K requests/sec
Redis Cluster distributes keys, not individual requests for one key.
Therefore:
product:viral
maps to one hash slot.
That slot belongs to one primary.
So:
500K req/sec
      ↓
one key
      ↓
one slot
      ↓
one primary
Adding more Redis nodes doesn't automatically solve this.
This is why hot keys are different from insufficient cluster-wide capacity.
________________________________________
16.31 Hot-key mitigation
Possible architecture:
              Product
                 |
        +--------+--------+
        |                 |
   Local Caffeine       Redis
        |                 |
        +--------+--------+
                 |
                DB
Or carefully designed replication/read strategies where appropriate.
Another option is key replication/sharding, but this complicates writes and consistency.
For a read-heavy immutable-ish value:
product:viral:1
product:viral:2
product:viral:3
can distribute reads across shards, but the application must know how to choose among them and keep values synchronized.
Don't do this unless the hot key is actually the bottleneck.
________________________________________
16.32 Cluster capacity vs hot-key capacity
This distinction is interview gold.
Suppose:
Redis Cluster capacity = 1M ops/sec
But one key receives:
400K ops/sec
on one shard.
Adding:
10 more Redis nodes
may not solve the problem.
Because:
one key
 ↓
one slot
 ↓
one primary
So you need:
hot-key mitigation
not merely:
more nodes
________________________________________
16.33 Cache hit ratio
For cache systems, monitor:
cache hits
cache misses
Example:
100K requests

95K hits
5K misses
Hit ratio:
95%
Seems good.
But don't celebrate blindly.
Suppose:
5K misses
all hit a database query taking:
200 ms
while hits take:
2 ms
That 5% could still dominate backend load.
Also measure hit ratio by key/workload, not just globally.
________________________________________
16.34 Cache hit ratio can hide bad behavior
Imagine:
99% hit ratio
but the 1% misses are:
very expensive DB queries
Your database can still be overloaded.
Therefore monitor:
cache hit ratio
+
DB load
+
miss cost
together.
