4. Why is invalidation fundamentally difficult?
Let's start with the simplest possible system.
Client
   |
   v
Application
   |
   +------> Redis
   |
   +------> DB
Suppose we use:
User getUser(long id) {
    User cached = redis.get("user:" + id);

    if (cached != null) {
        return cached;
    }

    User user = db.find(id);

    redis.set("user:" + id, user);

    return user;
}
And:
void updateUser(User user) {
    db.update(user);

    redis.delete("user:" + user.id());
}
Looks perfect.
But now ask the university-level question:
What happens if multiple operations happen concurrently?
That's where the real problem appears.
________________________________________
5. The classic race condition
Suppose Redis currently contains:
user:123 = John
Two application threads execute concurrently.
Thread A — READ
GET user:123
Thread B — UPDATE
UPDATE DB user:123 = Mike
DELETE Redis user:123
Timeline:
Time →

A: GET Redis
              ↓
              gets "John"

B: UPDATE DB
              ↓
              DB = "Mike"

B: DELETE Redis
              ↓
              Redis = empty

A: returns "John"
We returned stale data.
That alone isn't necessarily surprising.
But now consider a nastier race.
________________________________________
6. The dangerous race: stale value gets written back
Initial state:
DB:
John

Redis:
John
Thread A wants to read.
Thread B updates the user.
Imagine A has already obtained the old value from DB because Redis missed.
Timeline:
             Thread A                  Thread B

              DB read
              John

                                      DB update
                                      Mike

                                      Redis delete

              Redis set
              John
Now:
DB:
Mike

Redis:
John   ← stale!
This is much worse.
The cache was invalidated correctly.
Then the stale reader repopulated the cache with obsolete data.
This is one of the most important cache-invalidation race conditions to understand.
________________________________________
7. Notice what actually happened
The problem isn't Redis.
The problem isn't even Java.
The problem is:
We have concurrent operations acting on different copies of the same logical data.
We have:
                logical User #123
                       |
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             ↓                   ↓
          Database             Cache
          version N             version ?
Once copies exist, you need some mechanism to answer:
"Is this copy still valid?"
And that question is surprisingly hard.
________________________________________
8. Why can't we just update the cache whenever DB changes?
You might say:
void updateUser(User user) {
    db.update(user);
    redis.set("user:" + user.id(), user);
}
Better?
Sometimes.
But now there are two writes:
DB write
Redis write
What if:
DB write succeeds
Redis write fails
Result:
DB:
Mike

Redis:
John
Again stale.
What if the opposite ordering?
redis.set(...);
db.update(...);
Now:
Redis:
Mike

DB:
John
If another request reads the cache, it sees:
Mike
even though the database still says:
John
Now the cache is ahead of the database.
That's another consistency problem.
________________________________________
9. "Okay, then use a transaction."
This is where distributed systems starts becoming interesting.
You might think:
BEGIN TRANSACTION

DB UPDATE
Redis UPDATE

COMMIT
But Redis and your database are usually different systems.
For example:
MySQL
Redis
They don't magically participate in the same local transaction.
You can build distributed transaction protocols such as 2PC, but now you've introduced:
•	coordination 
•	blocking 
•	failure handling 
•	recovery 
•	latency 
•	operational complexity 
And most high-scale systems don't want their cache update coupled that tightly to the database.
This leads to a fundamental architectural principle:
A cache is usually treated as a derived copy, not another source of truth.
That distinction is extremely important.
________________________________________
10. The database is the source of truth
Think of the system like this:
              SOURCE OF TRUTH
                     │
                     ▼
                  Database
                     │
             derived representation
                     │
                     ▼
                   Redis
Redis isn't authoritative.
It is essentially saying:
"I have a copy that I believe is probably still valid."
That word believe is the entire cache-consistency problem.
________________________________________
11. Cache invalidation is really a knowledge problem
Here's the deeper way to think about it.
Suppose the database changes:
DB:
price = 100
      ↓
price = 120
Who knows that Redis contains:
price = 100
Potentially:
Application A
Application B
Application C
Redis
CDN
local JVM caches
search index
API gateway
The database knows:
price = 120
But the database doesn't automatically know:
"There are 37 copies of this value somewhere in the world."
That's the core difficulty.
________________________________________
12. The cache itself doesn't know when the database changes
This is crucial.
Redis doesn't magically observe:
UPDATE product
SET price = 120;
Redis sees nothing.
From Redis's perspective:
price = 100
is perfectly valid.
Therefore somebody has to communicate:
"Hey Redis, user:123 changed."
That communication can come from:
Application code
Application
