40. The deepest lesson
Distributed invalidation is not really about deleting cache keys.
It is about:
Propagating knowledge of state changes from an authoritative system to all derived copies, despite failures, duplication, delay, and reordering.
Once you understand that sentence, technologies become implementation choices:
Redis Pub/Sub
Kafka
RabbitMQ
CDC
Outbox
Streams
SNS/SQS
etc.
They're mechanisms for propagating change.
The underlying problem stays the same.
________________________________________
41. One final mental model
Think of your database as saying:
"I know the latest truth."
The cache says:
"I have a copy of what I believe the truth was."
The event system says:
"Something changed. Here's evidence that the truth moved forward."
The consumer says:
"Okay, I'll stop trusting my old copy."
TTL says:
"And even if communication fails,
I won't trust this copy forever."
That's distributed cache invalidation.
